           This is an application for discharging the rule for non-
compliance of the order of this court dated 28.09.2025.  
Mr. Mohammad Ali, learned Advocate for the respondent No. 3-
applicant by filing an application submits that at the time of issuance of the 
rule the court passed an interim order of stay of the auction process on 
condition that the petitioner shall pay 10% of the total outstanding dues 
within one month to the concerned Bank, failing which the rule shall stand 
discharged. The learned Advocate further submits that as per the order of the 
court, the petitioner did not pay 10% of the total outstanding dues which is 
gross violation, rendering the rule is liable to be discharged due to the non-
compliance of the court’s order. 
We have heard the learned Advocate for the respondent No. 3-
applicant bank. We have gone through the application and the relevant order 
dated 28.09.2025. 

<<PAGE 2>>
2 
 
It appears that the petitioner was directed to pay 10% of the total 
outstanding dues within one month to the concerned Bank and it was in the 
order that on failure to comply with the order the rule shall stand discharged. 
According to the statement made by the respondent No. 3-applicant the 
petitioner failed to comply with the order of this court. As a result, in 
accordance with the terms of the order, the rule has now been discharged.  
In view of the facts and circumstances, we are inclined to allow the 
instant application and accordingly, the same is allowed. 
 Resultantly, the rule is discharged for non-compliance of the court's 
order dated 28.09.2025 with cost of Tk. 10,000/-. The petitioner is directed 
to pay the said amount in the Account being No. 4435401017179 (savings 
account) maintained in the name of the Registrar General & Marshal of 
Supreme Court of Bangladesh, in default, the respondent bank will charge 
the said amount in the loan account of the petitioner and upon recovery of 
the dues will remit the said amount in the account mentioned above. 
 
                                                      (Sikder Mahmudur Razi, J.) 
 
 
                                                                   (Raziuddin Ahmed, J.) 
","The PETITIONER initiated writ proceedings against the STATE and other RESPONDENTS concerning an auction process for recovery of outstanding loan dues. By an order dated 28.09.2025, the court issued a Rule and temporarily stayed the auction, subject to payment of ten percent of the total outstanding amount to the creditor bank within one month. The order specified termination of the Rule if that condition was not fulfilled. RESPONDENT No. 3, the creditor bank, subsequently applied for termination of the Rule, stating that the required payment had not been made. Its application relies on the PETITIONER’S asserted non-compliance with the conditional interim order and seeks closure of the writ proceedings on that basis.",Not stated,REFUSED,"Reviewing the order dated 28.09.2025, the court identified an express condition requiring the PETITIONER to deposit ten percent of the outstanding dues within one month. That order also specified the consequence of non-payment. The court accepted the creditor RESPONDENT’S statement that no payment had been made and found the condition unfulfilled. It therefore applied the consequence already embedded in the interim order. The decision rested on non-compliance with that condition, not on an examination of the substantive merits of the auction dispute or any statutory provision.",9e3986d202dc5793d7cad487b2d5fcc67d8bc9e9dea3f5349de35abeebd1e8b5
