Kabir/BO 
","The PETITIONERS, defendants in a partition suit, challenge the appellate judgment and decree dated 29.10.2023 concerning their separate allotment of jointly owned land. Their revision invokes section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure. They claim 33.67 decimals through successive inheritance, sales, a family partition and an exchange deed, whereas the appellate court allotted them twelve decimals while recognizing possession over twenty-eight decimals. They argue that registered title documents and testimony establish the larger holding and that the lower courts misapprehended the evidence. The OPPOSITE PARTIES, who sought partition of 28.50 decimals through purchase and inheritance, maintain that the existing allotment is sufficient and oppose any enlargement. The controversy concerns the quantity of land attributable to each side and the evidentiary basis for separate possession following partition.",Not stated,PARTLY,"UNCERTAIN: The judgment revises the partition allocation on documentary and oral evidence without identifying a substantive statute as the basis of decision. Tracing the admitted original holding through inheritance, sales, acquisition deductions and exchange, the court found that the vendor could convey only 31.25 decimals, despite deeds describing 33.67 decimals. A previously missing sale deed admitted as additional evidence supported that chain of title. The PETITIONERS therefore established ownership and possession of 31.25 decimals, making the appellate allocation of twelve decimals inadequate, but did not establish their entire claimed quantity.",25e7c65b49d20e4ce1206a521872fe2a411a72c981de206923e07a3261d1dc85
