","The PETITIONER, a borrower, challenges an auction notice dated 30.09.2024 issued by a private creditor bank under section 12(3) of the Artha Rin Adalat Ain, 2003 for sale of mortgaged property. When the Rule issued, interim protection depended on repayment of the entire outstanding loan in eight equal instalments over twenty-four months. The creditor RESPONDENTS now seek termination of the proceedings, stating that the first instalment due by 27.01.2025 remained unpaid and no compliance affidavit was filed. They additionally argue that a writ against a private bank is not maintainable and that inclusion of the STATE and central-bank authorities was merely a device to invoke Article 102 of the Constitution. The immediate application concerns alleged breach of the repayment conditions accompanying the interim arrangement.",Not stated,REFUSED,"The court reviewed the conditional order and found that continued operation of the Rule depended on repayment through eight instalments within twenty-four months. The creditor RESPONDENTS stated that the PETITIONER had not paid the first instalment by the specified date. Treating that failure as a breach of the express condition, the court gave effect to the automatic consequence stipulated when the Rule issued. Although maintainability against a private bank was argued, the court’s own reasoning rested on non-compliance with the repayment schedule rather than a determination of that constitutional objection.",356aa904e827108998452f83d72200c6b7838706ad62b82221b146bef9932d50
