","The PETITIONER, a third-party purchaser of four decimals of land, challenges the appellate order dated 17.06.2025 declining to add him as a RESPONDENT in a partition appeal. He traces his purchase through an intermediate buyer to co-sharer defendants who received a joint allotment, and seeks separate allotment from their portion without reducing the plaintiff-OPPOSITE PARTIES’ claimed share. He argues that joinder would prevent further litigation. The plaintiff-OPPOSITE PARTIES respond that the vendors’ joint allotment remains undisturbed and the appeal concerns only their claimed share. They contend that the PETITIONER must look to his vendors’ portion and is neither necessary nor proper to the appeal. The lower courts treated the purchases as pending-litigation transfers under section 52 of the Transfer of Property Act. The revision under section 115 of the Code of Civil Procedure concerns joinder and separate partition relief.",Transfer of Property Act 1882,REFUSED,"The court treated the purchases as pending-litigation transfers and adopted the lis-pendens reasoning applied below. It found that the appeal concerned only the plaintiff-OPPOSITE PARTIES’ claimed enlargement of their share and did not challenge the joint allotment made to the PETITIONER’S vendors’ group. His purchase was described by definite boundaries and accompanied by possession, rather than continuing joint possession requiring another partition. He could realize the purchased portion from the land already allotted to his vendors, who had not appealed. The pending-litigation transfers therefore did not make his joinder necessary. The appellate court had correctly assessed the limited scope of the appeal and the availability of relief through the vendors’ existing allotment.",25420178351141f3fbeb6540910962c79067d5f898b292548f24b7e17ab3ac55
