 I agree. 
 
","The PETITIONERS were pursuing a long-pending civil revision against a municipal-authority OPPOSITE PARTY, but the judgment does not identify the underlying dispute, the order under challenge or the substantive relief sought. During the revision, several PETITIONERS died, requiring substitution of their heirs. Their advocate requested time for that purpose, and further opportunities were provided. A conditional order dated 02.11.2025 required a substitution application within the specified period and attached a consequence to non-compliance. No application was filed, and no representative appeared to explain progress. Neither side appeared at the final hearing. The matter concerns continued prosecution of the revision following those deaths and compliance with the court’s procedural direction.",Not stated,REFUSED,"The court relied on the PETITIONERS’ repeated failure to take substitution steps despite several adjournments and an express default condition. Their advocate had identified the need to substitute heirs, yet no application followed within the additional time. The court considered indefinite continuation of the old proceeding unjustified and treated the inaction as indicating loss of interest. It therefore gave effect to the consequence specified in the order dated 02.11.2025. The judgment contains no assessment of the underlying merits and identifies no statute governing its procedural decision.",197bcb3f88f2cbdbc58f8ee8788d80e74679ea98fecf369ae52a5da8449a2ddf
