","The PETITIONER sought directions against the STATE and banking RESPONDENTS to determine his applications under section 49(1)(Cha) of the Banking Companies Act, 2023 and to invalidate an auction notice concerning mortgaged property issued under the Artha Rin Adalat Ain, 2003. The bank RESPONDENTS sought termination of the Rule, stating that the auction had been completed, a corrected notice published, a sale deed executed and possession transferred. Relying on section 12(8) of that Ain, they argued that the purchaser’s title was valid and any auction irregularity sounded in damages. They also alleged concealment of those events, the PETITIONER’S conviction under the Penal Code and Prevention of Corruption Act, and doubts about his affidavit. The PETITIONER did not appear to oppose the application.",Not stated,REFUSED,"The court found that the PETITIONER had obtained the Rule and interim protection without disclosing material facts already apparent from the record. It characterized that nondisclosure as a fraud upon the court and accepted the bank RESPONDENTS’ account of the completed auction, registered transfer and delivery of possession. The concealment undermined the basis for equitable constitutional relief. The court’s own reasoning did not interpret the Banking Companies Act or the auction statute, instead treating the misleading presentation of the proceedings and surrounding circumstances as sufficient to terminate the Rule.",566acbe5185cb1575acc791cc2ce347dd9c1f79d6936040a53e699098ff2b052
