","The PETITIONER, an office-holder of a political association, sought registration of the association as a political party and allocation of an electoral symbol against the election-authority RESPONDENTS. The original and supplementary Rules concerned the decisions dated 11.06.2018 and 21.10.2018 on his registration and reconsideration applications. He asserted compliance with Article 90B of the Representation of the People Order, 1972 and the Political Parties Registration Rules, 2008, including constitutional amendments, internal democracy, women’s representation and organizational presence. He argued that the documents established the required committees and membership, that earlier deficiencies had been corrected, and that no specific concerns or hearing preceded the adverse decisions. He invoked Articles 38 and 102 of the Constitution, challenging the decisions as arbitrary, discriminatory and restrictive of associational rights.",Other: Representation of the People Order 1972,ALLOWED,"The court found substantial compliance with the Representation of the People Order and registration rules: the amended party constitution contained the required safeguards, while uncontroverted records established sufficient organizational presence and membership. The initial deficiencies had been corrected at the election authority’s request. Vague objections, unsupported by persisting defects, could not justify non-registration. The absence of specific notice and a fair opportunity to clarify breached natural justice. The court held that these extralegal obstacles disproportionately interfered with Article 38 associational rights and exceeded the authority’s regulatory powers, making judicial intervention under Article 102 appropriate.",1b8eb22e95fc13046d3a9a0181407dafb83683e324845519f884c414c50b3e0d
