","The PETITIONER, a plaintiff in a title suit, challenges the appellate order dated 26.10.2023 concerning refusal of a temporary injunction over a small disputed portion of land. She seeks preservation of possession, relying on registered purchase deeds, city-survey recording and an alleged attempt at forcible dispossession. Her application invokes Order 39, rules 1 and 2 read with section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure, and she argues that a prima facie case and the balance of convenience favour protective relief. The defendant OPPOSITE PARTY relies on an earlier registered deed, long possession, proportional reduction of recorded areas in the latest survey and allegations that one supporting deed was fabricated. The revision concerns interim protection pending determination of title and the appropriate extent of any status-quo direction.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"UNCERTAIN: The final order preserves possession of the disputed fraction rather than expressly restoring the injunction application, so PARTLY reflects limited protective relief. The court found the PETITIONER’S title chain incomplete because the plaint did not explain how two asserted vendors acquired the interests they conveyed. It therefore identified no prima facie case. Nevertheless, the risk of unlawful dispossession made preservation of the existing situation necessary. The court confined that protection to the small portion actually in dispute, excluded the broader plaint schedule, and directed an expedited trial.",f386b0052b05dbe4bc7b78f06af128fb1e2e5c376ebdad55a7ea1a494db0a40c
