","The PETITIONER sought directions under Article 102 of the Constitution requiring the STATE and environmental authorities to stop an allegedly unauthorized brick kiln operated by a private RESPONDENT. She alleged that the kiln’s location and operations endangered the environment, forest, air quality and local health, and relied on the Environment Conservation Act, 1995 and the Brick Manufacturing and Kiln Establishment (Control) Act, 2013. She sought a declaration that official inaction lacked lawful authority and orders for closure or other protective measures. The private RESPONDENT relied on licences, permissions and an environmental clearance certificate. The proceeding also concerns the effect of administrative action on the clearance and a pending appeal by the kiln operator.",Not stated,REFUSED,"UNCERTAIN: The Rule was disposed of as infructuous after the clearance certificate was cancelled, rather than by an express closure order, so REFUSED reflects the absence of an operative declaration or direction. The court found that the cancellation accomplished the purpose of the representation and left the Rule without practical efficacy. It did not adjudicate the statutory allegations afresh. Because the kiln operator’s appeal against cancellation remained pending, the court preserved the appellate authority’s freedom to decide that appeal independently and in accordance with law.",1b7f1591e9f516dd7ff0bfdd9d445b967a7675a80e50224307201d4be1353bce
