","The PETITIONERS, defendants in a suit for permanent injunction, challenge the appellate decree dated 12.06.2014 concerning interference with the plaintiff OPPOSITE PARTY’S irrigation operations. The OPPOSITE PARTY relies on a licence from the irrigation committee, an electricity connection and longstanding water supply, alleging that the PETITIONERS installed competing tubewells without permission. The PETITIONERS contend that their machines served local residents, that one was licensed, and that the OPPOSITE PARTY’S inadequate irrigation prompted community objections. They argue that the courts below misappreciated the evidence. The revision under section 115 of the Code of Civil Procedure concerns the scope of interference with concurrent factual findings and whether unauthorized installation of tubewells justified injunctive protection of the licensed operator.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court emphasized the limited character of revisional jurisdiction: concurrent factual findings are not reopened merely because another view is possible. The PETITIONERS produced no licence or official record proving authorization for their tubewells, while the plaintiff OPPOSITE PARTY held a formal irrigation licence. Under the applicable irrigation regulation, installation without prior permission was a legal wrong infringing the licensed operator’s rights. The courts below had assessed the oral and documentary evidence without perversity, misreading or jurisdictional error. Their concurrent findings therefore supplied no basis for revisional interference.",da5e1fe7868cb10491f5571e2be4dbf9a987a3850cdd53617f57b69c34a26b4f
