","The PETITIONER, a defendant in a later civil suit, challenges the order dated 23.03.2017 concerning applications to stay that suit and to declare it abated. She invokes section 10 of the Code of Civil Procedure, arguing that an earlier suit between the same parties concerns the same subject matter. She also invokes Order 22, rule 4(3) read with section 151, contending that a defendant died without substitution. The plaintiff OPPOSITE PARTY responds that the suits differ in reliefs, issues, parties and land, and that the deceased defendant’s name had already been struck out. The revision under section 115(1) concerns the prerequisites for staying a later suit, the effect of the death on the proceedings and the adequacy of the trial court’s order.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court held that section 10 requires directly and substantially identical issues, the same parties or their representatives litigating under the same title, and a competent earlier forum. The two suits sought different declarations, involved different records and land measurements, and did not have wholly identical parties, so a stay was unavailable. Although the trial court’s order was not fully reasoned, its direction for simultaneous trial necessarily denied a stay and was sustainable under the court’s inherent power to avoid complexity. The abatement argument also failed because the deceased defendant’s name had already been struck out.",9fba0a03a8a8628b876cd9bf51b91658fb060c4dbf5649889d61c2b61d906c1b
