","The PETITIONER, a husband and father, challenges the appellate decree dated 15.12.2021 concerning unpaid dower and maintenance owed to his wife and children, the OPPOSITE PARTIES. He argues that divorce had become effective through the Arbitration Council, ending the OPPOSITE PARTY’S maintenance entitlement, and that he had paid deferred dower. He also contends that equal monthly amounts for adults and minors were excessive and that the annual enhancement lacked a rational basis. The OPPOSITE PARTIES rely on the marriage contract, consummation, the PETITIONER’S acknowledged obligations and his failure to maintain the family. The revision concerns the surviving effect of divorce on dower and maintenance, the quantum of child maintenance and the reasonableness of periodic increases.",Other: Dissolution of Muslim Marriage Act 1939,PARTLY,"The court held that consummation and the signed marriage contract established the PETITIONER’S obligation to pay unpaid dower, and that dissolution of marriage did not extinguish the OPPOSITE PARTY’S dower rights under section 5 of the Dissolution of Muslim Marriage Act, 1939. The children remained entitled to maintenance. The court accepted the concurrent findings on arrears and regular maintenance but considered the appellate annual enhancement excessive. It therefore reduced the children’s yearly increase from fifteen percent to ten percent while otherwise leaving the decree undisturbed.",8bd4c11430b0f9e66af71bbc7aaafc2360b7f19c409a6494a9ac26b1fe85a5b7
