The learned Advocate Mr. Kamal Ziaul Islam appearing on behalf of the 
respondent Nos. 11 and 12 submits that earlier the petitioner Aysha Siddika filed Writ 
Petition No. 11130 of 2016 regarding absorbtion in her service on the same ground 
and after hearing, the petitioner not pressed the Rule issued in the said writ petition 
and by order dated 31.05.2017, the High Court Division discharged the Rule for non-
prosecution. Against the said order passed by the High Court Division, the petitioner 
filed Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal No. 2471 of 2017 which was also dismissed 
for default. 
In view of the order dated 31.05.2017 passed in Writ Petition No. 11130 of 
2016 and Civil Petition for Leave to Appeal No. 2471 of 2017, the Rule is not 
maintainable.  
We do not find any substance in the Rule.  
In the result, the Rule is discharged.  
However, there will be no order as to costs. 
 
Dihider Masum Kabir, J. 
I agree. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
","The PETITIONERS invoked Article 102 of the Constitution seeking relief concerning absorption into service. The private RESPONDENTS oppose the proceeding as maintainable only if it can overcome earlier litigation on the same ground. They state that one PETITIONER previously brought a constitutional petition seeking absorption, did not press that Rule, and the proceeding terminated for non-prosecution; a subsequent application for leave to appeal also ended for default. No one appears for the PETITIONERS at the final hearing. The dispute concerns whether the present Rule can be entertained despite those earlier procedural terminations and whether it discloses any substance warranting relief.",Not stated,REFUSED,"The court relied on the earlier constitutional proceeding in which the same PETITIONER sought absorption on the same ground but did not press the Rule, leading to its termination for non-prosecution. The subsequent leave application also ended for default. Those prior procedural outcomes made the present Rule unmaintainable. The court found no substance in the renewed challenge and declined to grant relief. Its reasoning rested on the history of the earlier proceedings rather than on an interpretation of any named statute.",5bee3425f43cf25f0924f930483d35fb0952063e67017e78e2d757866f61b554
