","The APPELLANT in the lead appeal, a police officer, challenges the conviction and sentence dated 09.09.2020 under section 15(2) of the Torture and Custodial Death (Prevention) Act, 2013 for custodial torture causing death. Connected APPELLANTS challenge related convictions under section 15. The prosecution relies on victim and eyewitness accounts, a judicial inquiry, photographic identification, inquest and post-mortem materials, and the special evidentiary rule in section 19. The APPELLANTS invoke the Code of Criminal Procedure and contest the inquiry, identification method, delay, medical opinion and individual participation. The STATE and complainant RESPONDENT support the convictions, arguing that the special Act governs and that custodial torture caused fatal cardiac failure. The appeals concern the lawfulness of the trial process and proof of custodial torture and death.",Other: Torture and Custodial Death (Prevention) Act 2013,REFUSED,"UNCERTAIN: The judgment decides three connected appeals; REFUSED is assessed for the lead appeal of the police officer whose conviction was affirmed. The court held that the special Act permitted judicial inquiry where police investigation was distrusted, and that photographic identification was reliable in the circumstances. Victim and eyewitness evidence, the inquest, hospital visits and the sequence of custody established severe torture. The court discounted the doctor’s opinion that injuries did not cause death, finding that physical and psychological trauma produced fatal cardiac failure. The special evidentiary rule and uncontroverted evidence supported the lead APPELLANT’S conviction.",ec6cfc1cfc88973df7e1b0be19eb8c51c1147aee0812c33522d859a201d5c38d
