(Fatema Najib)","The PETITIONER, a government contractor, challenged multiple debarment decisions uploaded on the electronic procurement portal by the procuring-entity RESPONDENT, which excluded his firm from tender participation for varying periods. He alleged that fabricated certificates entered through departmental database systems caused the debarments, that no written orders were communicated, and that departmental proceedings were pending against officials. He sought leniency and relied on the Public Procurement Act, 2006 and Public Procurement Rules, 2008. The procuring-entity RESPONDENT alleged manipulated completion dates in tender submissions, constituting fraudulent practice under rule 127, and invoked the e-GP Guidelines, 2011 and alternative review remedies. The proceeding concerns the lawfulness and proportionality of portal-wide debarment.",Other: Public Procurement Act 2006,PARTLY,"The court accepted that the impugned debarments had operated across the electronic procurement portal for substantial periods and that the PETITIONER had already suffered significant exclusion. Although the RESPONDENT alleged manipulated completion dates amounting to fraudulent practice, the court treated the mistakes as warranting a lenient approach in light of the PETITIONER’S apology, undertaking and prior suffering. Following an Appellate Division approach that debarment could be treated as served out, it commuted the remaining periods to the time already undergone and permitted future participation subject to ordinary eligibility.",1148641420a5a01e6e85cffb35612631f703c7a25a4a4cc23d00f713b4949d55
