     (Fatema Najib, J) 
","The PETITIONER sought a direction under Article 102 of the Constitution requiring mutation of land in her name, relying on a heba deed and an earlier ministry order directing mutation. She alleged that the land had been wrongly recorded in the government’s name and that the authorities failed to implement the mutation direction. A private RESPONDENT opposed the claim, stating that the land was lawfully listed as abandoned property, sold to the RESPONDENT’S predecessor, and that the Court of Settlement and the Appellate Division had already determined the property’s status. The proceeding concerns the effect of those earlier determinations, the PETITIONER’S disclosure of material facts and the availability of writ relief for mutation.",Not stated,REFUSED,"The court found that the Appellate Division had already settled the status of the property in earlier appeals concerning the same land. The PETITIONER sought mutation while suppressing that binding determination and other material facts, and the writ petition contained inconsistencies between its statements and annexures. Applying the principle that fraud vitiates everything, the court treated the nondisclosure as destroying the legal basis for discretionary constitutional relief. It therefore declined to direct mutation and left the earlier adjudication of the property’s abandoned status undisturbed.",a0b7317ab45adb5297cf5f17975c31f6e553d0d16329f61b2804d998f441b0a7
