(Fatema Najib, J)","The PETITIONER, a fishermen’s cooperative society, challenged the rejection of its request to waive or adjust lease money for a jalmahal and to extend its lease for one year as compensation for losses caused by earth filling. It relied on inquiry reports confirming that a substantial portion of the fishery had been filled and on earlier representations to the land authorities. Supplementary Rules challenged a subsequent lease tender and a certificate demand for unpaid lease money. The STATE RESPONDENTS and an added RESPONDENT lessee relied on expiry of the PETITIONER’S lease, the subsequent lease and delivery of possession, and the lease agreement’s terms. The proceeding concerns compensation, lease extension, unpaid lease money and recovery under the Public Demand Recovery Act, 1913.",Other: Public Demand Recovery Act 1913,REFUSED,"UNCERTAIN: The refusal rested mainly on the lease terms and expiry, while the Public Demand Recovery Act, 1913 was named in relation to recovery of unpaid lease money. The court found that the PETITIONER’S lease had expired, the added RESPONDENT held a valid subsequent lease and possession, and the PETITIONER was no longer in possession. Clause 27 of the lease barred later claims for extension or adjustment based on earlier promises. The PETITIONER had enjoyed the fishery under interim orders without paying the final year’s lease money, so the recovery decision and refusal of compensation disclosed no illegality.",7f9d0c3c690fdf0861ac5fae61ea6c2824017d37290dd9ba217f783d22d15fd6
