(Fatema Najib, J)","The PETITIONERS, commercial tenants of premises owned by a statutory-body RESPONDENT, challenged notices requiring them to execute lease agreements at a higher rate within three days. They sought execution of lease deeds at the earlier rate, relying on long possession, prior renewals, rent payments and substantial investment. They asserted that the lessor’s powers derived from the Bangladesh Industrial Enterprises (Nationalization) Order, 1972, now the Bangladesh Shilpa Protisthan Jatiyokoron Ain, 2018. The RESPONDENT argued that the tenancy was a private bilateral contract renewable on fresh terms, and that disputes belonged before civil courts under the Contract Act, 1872 or the Rent Control Premises Act, 1991. The proceeding concerns the maintainability of writ relief for alleged breach of a non-statutory tenancy agreement.",Not stated,REFUSED,"The court examined the tenancy agreement and found it to be a private contract governed by its own terms, not a statutory contract. No statute was shown to require the lessor to enter into the agreement on prescribed terms or to impose the obligations sought to be enforced. Applying the Appellate Division’s distinction between sovereign or statutory contracts and ordinary commercial contracts, the court held that the dispute did not meet the conditions for constitutional writ enforcement. The PETITIONERS’ remedies, if any, lay in the contractual or civil forums rather than in the Rule.",a457d61498f9075b4cb5f1fedaebb91500f7bd2b0be6e2a10aa919ea4097da1f
