     (Fatema Najib, J) 
 
","The PETITIONERS, elected office-bearers of a co-operative society, challenged an appellate award dated 06.03.2024 that reversed an arbitrator’s award and directed re-election for three posts. They relied on the election committee’s conduct of the poll, the absence of contemporaneous allegations, and the arbitrator’s rejection of the election disputes. They argued that a partial re-election was impermissible and that Article 102 of the Constitution was available because no question of law arose. The private RESPONDENTS, unsuccessful candidates, relied on sections 50, 52 and 55 of the Co-operative Society Act, 2001, contending that an equally efficacious statutory remedy lay before the District Judge. The proceeding concerns maintainability and exhaustion of that remedy.",Other: Co-operative Society Act 2001,REFUSED,"The court held that the Co-operative Society Act provided a specific statutory route for challenging the appellate award, including review by the District Judge on questions of law and fact. Article 102(2) barred writ relief where an equally efficacious remedy existed, subject to exceptions such as fundamental-rights infringement or challenge to the vires of a law. The PETITIONERS had not invoked that remedy and showed no exceptional ground for bypassing it. The writ was therefore unmaintainable, regardless of the merits of the election dispute.",f433463520214d928618c805e8c93b2d16a550da53606d0bcd1a48a0ea797d57
1500-1518,Civil Revision No. 31 OF 2008,2025-03-10,"
