     (Md. Hamidur Rahman, J:) 
","The PETITIONERS, plaintiffs in a title suit, challenge the appellate order dated 04.03.2007 concerning the STATE’S application to reopen an ex parte decree and retry the suit. They argue that summons was duly served on the Government pleader under Order XXVII, rule 4 of the Code of Civil Procedure, and that the STATE’S application under Order IX, rule 13 was barred by delay. The STATE responds that the named defendant was the Deputy Commissioner of another district, service on the local Government pleader was ineffective, the decree was collusive, and the land was vested property. The revision under section 115 concerns proper service, proof of collusion and the limitation governing restoration of an ex parte decree.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court held that service on the local Government pleader was not effective against the Deputy Commissioner of another district who was the named government defendant. Once service was denied, the burden lay on the PETITIONERS to prove proper service by cogent evidence, which they failed to discharge. The record indicated collusion and suppression in obtaining the ex parte decree over vested property. Limitation did not bar the restoration application where fraud appeared, and official delay could be condoned. The appellate court’s decision therefore disclosed no infirmity warranting revisional interference.",be3075a1c76f36ff60267c45cfbdecb3b9d918052161f547843444545b1890d6
