","The PETITIONERS, plaintiffs in a title suit, challenge the appellate order dated 23.07.2003 concerning refusal of a temporary injunction over land they claim by inheritance and tree planting. They rely on C.S. and S.A. records, a local inspection report and the balance of convenience. The STATE OPPOSITE PARTIES contend that the land is river-accreted khas land recorded in the government’s name and used for an ideal-village project. The revision under section 115 of the Code of Civil Procedure concerns prima facie title, possession, demarcation and equitable interim protection while the title suit remains pending.",Not stated,REFUSED,"The court found that the PETITIONERS had obtained and long enjoyed an interim status-quo order over government property while the title suit remained pending. It treated that conduct as inconsistent with clean hands and discretionary injunctive relief. The concurrent findings below had identified no prima facie case or established possession in the PETITIONERS, particularly against the government record and the ideal-village use. The court therefore declined to disturb the refusal of temporary injunction and directed an expedited trial.",5a9077ea3176313925b55678fa549b6e794837f623249bc8135596458d7ad1c7
