concerned, at once.  
 
Debasish Roy Chowdhury, J: 
I agree. 
","The PETITIONER, the son of a detenue, invoked Article 102 of the Constitution challenging a detention order dated 21.04.2025 issued under section 3(2) of the Special Powers Act, 1974 and its extension. He alleged that the order disclosed no specific ground, that the detenue had already obtained bail in the only mentioned case, and that continued custody rested on surmise rather than lawful satisfaction. He also relied on an earlier arrest under section 54 of the Code of Criminal Procedure and on fundamental-rights protections. The STATE RESPONDENTS neither appeared nor produced the detenue or explained any other pending case. The proceeding concerns the legality of preventive detention and the detenue’s liberty.",Special Powers Act 1974,ALLOWED,"The court examined the detention order and found that it identified no reason beyond the particular criminal case in which the detenue had already obtained bail. The RESPONDENTS failed to appear, produce the detenue, or provide any information about other pending cases justifying continued custody. The extension of detention therefore lacked a disclosed lawful basis. In the absence of any answer to the Rule or evidence of other lawful custody, the court held the detention and its extension illegal and directed release unless the detenue was wanted in another case.",e9b6e5cb26c5ca2aacdb101ddc72751bafcb823ce796e971cfcf5c3fa40a2082
