at once.  
  
              
 
Helal-ABO     
","The PETITIONERS, purchasers of property, challenge orders refusing to reject a pre-emption petition at the threshold under Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure. They contend that the vendors acquired the property by an oral gift from their mother, leaving the pre-emptor OPPOSITE PARTIES without co-sharer status or cause of action, and that the sale deed’s registration was incomplete under section 60 of the Registration Act. The pre-emptor OPPOSITE PARTIES claim an inherited one-third share under Muslim law and seek pre-emption under section 24 of the Non-Agricultural Tenancy Act, arguing that the alleged oral gift requires trial. The revision concerns whether disputed co-sharership and registration completion justify summary rejection.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court held that the asserted oral gift was a disputed fact requiring evidence and could not be conclusively resolved on an application for rejection of the petition. Until the gift was proved, the pre-emptor OPPOSITE PARTIES’ claimed inherited share could not be treated as extinguished. The court also reasoned that a transfer takes effect on execution and registration, while section 24 of the Non-Agricultural Tenancy Act ties pre-emption to transfer or notice, not to the later ministerial completion of registration under section 60. The grounds therefore did not satisfy Order VII, rule 11, and the pre-emption case required trial.",fcc7f2ff28bae8d92971c533959c893a353db627b6749fc756eb6b44dda5ed41
1500-1524,Civil Revision No.1857 of 2024,2025-11-04,"
