","The PETITIONER challenged an office order dated 16.08.2015 rejecting his appeal under section 15 of the Building Construction Act, 1952 and confirming notices requiring removal of alleged unauthorized construction. He relied on an approved building plan, ownership documents, a pending specific-performance suit and the absence of a hearing before the appellate authority. He also objected to a new ground concerning a detailed area plan and hill land appearing for the first time in the appellate order. The development-authority RESPONDENT relied on complaints of encroachment and hill cutting, inspection materials and sections 3B and 6 of the Act. The proceeding concerns procedural fairness and the lawfulness of the removal notices and appellate rejection.",Other: Building Construction Act 1952,ALLOWED,"The court found that the first complaint had effectively been dropped after the PETITIONER supplied ownership documents. The later removal notices did not comply with the show-cause and hearing requirements of section 3B(2) and (3). Although section 15 did not expressly require a hearing, natural justice required one before the appeal was decided. The appellate order also introduced a detailed-area-plan and hill-land ground absent from the notices and approval, and no inspection established construction beyond the allotted plot. The notices and appellate rejection therefore lacked a lawful procedural and factual basis.",d6d55a9bb1d5572fe6e4ad6c24d0c81d15a31590f9279fa1021abe1d96e34360
