affidavit evidence.  
The purpose of verification of affidavit, he goes to contend, is to 
enable the court to find out which of the facts can be said to be proved by 
affidavit evidence of rival parties. In this regard he argues that facts may be 
true to knowledge, or true to information received from a person, or may be 
based on some record or document. However, unless the source is separately 
disclosed, it is difficult for the court to test, either the truth, or authenticity, 
or genuineness of the allegation made in the affidavit. The aim is to make 
the deponent responsible for the allegation made by him. In the absence of 
proper verification, affidavit cannot be admitted into evidence. Further, the 
compliance of verification rule is mandatory, and its breach in substance, 
will make so much of the statement in the affidavit as is not in compliance 
with verification rule, inadmissible. 
In addition, he argues, Rule 3 of Chapter-XIA of the Rules, 1973 
provides that every application/petition under Article 102 of the Constitution 
shall set out concisely the facts upon which the petitioner relies, the grounds 
upon which the Court is asked to issue the direction, order or declaration, his 
legal right in the matter in question, his demand of justice and the denial 
thereof and shall conclude with a prayer stating, as clearly as the 
circumstances permit, the exact nature of the relief or relieves sought for.   
In the present writ petition, he submits, the cause title failed to refer to 
the impugned cause of the writ petition. More so, there is no reference to any 
law which is alleged to have been violated by a public functionary in 
exercise of its public duties so as to invoke the powers of this Hon’ble Court 

<<PAGE 25>>
 
25
under Article 102 of the Constitution to issue writ of mandamus even in a 
public interest litigation. Instead of reference to an impugned cause or 
violation of law that a public functionary/statutory body failed to comply, 
the cause title refers to several directions on public functionary/statutory 
body about a private sector entity and an individual.  
Also, the instant writ petition, he goes to contend, is a two paragraph 
statement of facts which are not only  vague, unspecific, uncertain but also 
are based on wild conjectures and surmises with no specifics, particulars or 
even annexures. More so, the prayer is vague and in relation to unspecified 
and indeterminate number of entities under the guise of “companies of 
Beximco Group of Companies”. In the given context, issuance of Rule and 
passing interim order pursuant to such vague prayer is not only a violation of 
the basic principles of natural justice, but the same, on the face of it, is not 
grounded on any substance. In support he has referred the decision of the 
case of Ashok Paper Mills Kamgar Union v. Union of India and another 
reported in (2000) 7 SCC 326 (329).  
  
Accordingly, he ends his argument by submitting that this writ 
petition is not maintainable in its present form and manner. If the Hon’ble 
Court allows such petition, it would not only make the Rules, 1973 
redundant, it would open flood gates that the luminaries of the Bench once 
cautioned about. The necessity of discipline by following the Rules, 1973 in 
filing a writ petition is not only to ensure sanctity of the Courts but also, to 
ensure that there is no abuse of process, thereby causing injustice and that 
there is no violation of the principles of natural justice of the person alleged 
of some wrong doing.  

<<PAGE 26>>
 
26
As such, he submits that this Rule is liable to be discharged with 
costs.  
Our apex court while giving answer to the core question, whether the 
expression “any person aggrieved”, as occurring in Article 102(1) and (2)(a) 
of the Constitution of the People’s Republic of Bangladesh, should be 
liberated from the traditional view so far attributed to it, has observed, inter 
alia, in the case of Kazi Mukhlesur Rahman Vs. Bangladesh: 26 DLR(SC) 
44 
“It appears to us that the question of locus standi does not involve the 
Court's jurisdiction to hear a person but of the competency of the 
person to claim a hearing, so that the question is one of discretion 
which the Court exercises upon due consideration of the facts and 
circumstance of each case.” 
 
In the said case the Appellate Division ultimately settled the following 
general principles:  
 (1) the High Court Division does not suffer from any lack of 
jurisdiction under Article 102 to hear a person; 
 (2) The High Court Division will grant locus standi to a person who 
agitates a question affecting a constitutional issue of grave 
importance, posing a threat to his fundamental rights which pervade 
and extend to the entire territory of Bangladesh; 
 (3) If a fundamental right is involved, the impugned matter need not 
affect a purely personal right of the applicant touching him alone. It is 
enough if he shares that right in common with others; 
 (4) In interpreting the words ""any person aggrieved"", consideration 
of ""Fundamental Rights"" in Part III of the Constitution is a relevant 
one; 
 (5) It is the competency of the person to claim a hearing which is at 
the heart of the interpretation of the words ""any person aggrieved""; 
 (6) It is a question of exercise of discretion by the High Court 
Division as to whether it will treat that person aggrieved or not; and 

<<PAGE 27>>
 
27
 (7) The High Court Division will exercise that jurisdiction upon due 
consideration of the facts and circumstances of each case.” 
 
In Dr. Mohiuddin Farooque Vs. Bangladesh reported in 49DLR 
(AD)1, the Appellate Division while giving interpretation to the expression 
“any person aggrieved” approximates the test of “sufficient interest”, by 
observing, inter alia, “Any person other than an officious intervenor or a 
wayfarer without any interest or concern beyond what belongs to any of the 
120 million people of the country or a person with an oblique motive, having 
sufficient interest in the matter in dispute is qualified to be a person 
aggrieved and can maintain an action for judicial redress of public injury 
arising from breach of public duty or for violation of some provision of the 
Constitution or the law and seek enforcement of such public duty and 
observance of such constitutional or legal provision. Now what is 'sufficient 
interest' will essentially depend on the co-relation between the matter 
brought before the Court and the person who is bringing it. It is not possible 
to lay down any strait-jacket formula for determining sufficient interest 
which may be applicable in all cases. Of necessity the question has to be 
decided in the facts of each case ......... ” 
Further, it has been observed in the said case that “This is not to say 
that Article 102 has nationalised each person's cause as every other person's 
cause. The traditional view remains true, valid and effective till today in so 
far as individual rights and individual infraction thereof are concerned. But 
when a public injury or public wrong or infraction of a fundamental right 
affecting an indeterminate number of people is involved it is not necessary, 
in the scheme of our Constitution, that the multitude of individuals who has 
been collectively wronged or injured or whose collective fundamental rights 

<<PAGE 28>>
 
28
have been invaded are to invoke the jurisdiction under Article 102 in a 
multitude of individual writ petitions, each representing his own portion of 
concern. Insofar as it concerns public wrong or public injury or invasion of 
fundamental rights of an indeterminate number of people, any member of the 
public, being a citizen, suffering the common injury or common invasion in 
common with others or any citizen or an indigenous association, as 
distinguished from a local component of a foreign organisation, espousing 
that particular cause is a person aggrieved and has the right to invoke the 
jurisdiction under Article 102. 
It is, therefore, the cause that the citizen-applicant or the indigenous 
and native association espouses which will determine whether the applicant 
has the competency to claim a hearing or not. If he espouses a purely 
individual cause, he is a person aggrieved if his own interests are affected, If 
he espouses a public cause involving public wrong or public injury, he need 
not be personally affected. The public wrong or injury is very much a 
primary concern of the Supreme Court which in the scheme of our 
Constitution is a constitutional vehicle for exercising the judicial power of 
the people.” 
 In the instant case, the respondent no.3 has assailed the bonafides  as 
well as the fitness of the petitioner for invoking the discretionary jurisdiction 
of the High Court Division under Article 102 of the Constitution claiming to 
have embarked  on a “fishing expedition’’ for information basing on wild 
conjecture, 
vague, 
unspecified 
statements 
without any 
supporting 
documents, nor is well-founded on any material facts, matters and 
circumstances,  as provided under Chapter IV, IVA and XIA of the Supreme 
Court of Bangladesh (High Court Division) Rules, 1973; hence, it is devoid 

<<PAGE 29>>
 
29
of any substance whatsoever for consideration  by this Court and 
accordingly, it is liable to be discharged. 
     In the light of the observations and findings of the Appellate Division, as 
quoted above, let us now examine while exercising constitutional 
jurisdiction of this Court under Article 102 of the Constitution, the fitness of 
the petitioner, in other words, his “locus standi” claiming hearing on the 
issue of public cause involving public wrong or public injury.   
The petitioner, a practicing lawyer of this Court for several decades, 
known for espousing the cause of public issue involving compliance of the 
constitutional obligation by the State, including public wrong or public 
injury, has filed the instant writ petition  in the nature of mandamus seeking 
direction upon Bangladesh Bank, respondent no.1, to provide “information” 
on respondent No. 3 and all other affiliated  business concerns of Beximco 
Group of Companies of respondent  No.4 on how much “unpaid” loan 
respondent No.4 had obtained from all banks and financial institutions, 
present status of loans  and repayment and to appoint a ‘Receiver’ to manage 
the Beximco Group of Companies and also, to attach all properties 
belonging  to the said Group of Companies.  
  However, at the time of issuance of the Rule Nisi, vide an interim  
order dated 05.09.2024 this Court directed Bangladesh Bank, respondent 
no.1 to appoint a receiver to manage the respective companies of Beximco 
Group and to attach all its properties (belonging to said Group of 
Companies), but, for a prescribed period. Respondent no.1 was also directed 
to recover the money back to Bangladesh obtained by the respondent no.4 
from different banks.  

<<PAGE 30>>
 
30
          Said interim order was subsequently modified by the Appellate 
Division  in CPLA No.3174 of 2024 vide order dated 12.11.2024 retaining 
the order of  appointment of receiver and attachment of the properties of 
Beximco Group  of Companies except respondent  no.3, till disposal of the 
Rule.   
  In compliance of the said interim order Bangladesh Bank by filing 
5(five) sets of affidavits of compliance in particular dated 02.03.2025 has 
provided before this Court detailed informations on the present position of 
