Pharmaceutical Limited have control and influence over other companies of  

<<PAGE 33>>
 
33
Beximco Group as well their unpaid loans/liabilities involving public money 
and is at stake towards its recovery. 
   
 It is also disclosed from the reports filed by the Department of Offsite 
Supervision (DOS) and the Department of Banking Inspection-1 (DBI-1) of 
Bangladesh Bank that in the year of 2022, 4 companies of Beximco Group 
borrowed money from Janata Bank PLC, a government owned bank,  
exceeding Single Borrower Exposure Limit; in 2023, other  26 companies of 
the said Group of Companies have also exceeded the Single Borrower 
Exposure Limit while taking loan in flagrant violation of the Bank 
Companies Act, 1991. Furthermore, Beximco and its related companies did 
not repay the EDF Fund to Janata Bank PLC, at USD 117.88 on the 
completion of the respective period. 
Bangladesh Bank has also, collected a list of the banks and financial 
institutions who provided financial facilities to the respective companies of 
Beximco Group in the forms of loans, advances, L/Cs, Guarantees etc.  It is 
revealed therefrom that 16(sixteen) scheduled banks and 7(seven) financial 
institutions have granted loan facilities to the companies of Beximco Group 
(upto 30.09.2024) wherein Janata Bank PLC alone has granted loan to 29 
companies of Beximco Group, IFIC Bank granted loan to 29 companies, 
National Bank PLC granted loan to 9 companies, Sonali Bank PLC granted 
loan to 4 companies, Agrani Bank Ltd. granted loan to 4 companies, AB 
Bank Ltd. granted loan to 6 companies, Exim Bank Ltd. granted loan to 5 
companies and other banks granted loan to more than one companies 
belonging to Beximco Group. Thus, it goes to indicate that the respective 
companies of the said Group wanted to hide the actual beneficial owners that 
may lead to money laundering and tax evasion, in contrary to the prevailing 

<<PAGE 34>>
 
34
laws of the country. This practice of hiding the actual beneficial owners 
might have accelerated the breach of Single Borrower Exposure Limit, the 
limit which the banks always maintain to adhere in the light of Section 
26Kha of the Bank Companies Act, 1991. As per the report of Bangladesh 
Bank dated 31.12.2017, Beximco Limited committed irregularity by not 
paying the first installment as per the terms of the restructure of loan and had 
hidden the real status of classification of loans at the time of opening L/Cs; 
the loans of Beximco Computers Limited is not well covered by the 
collateral securities and no adequate insurance against the investment was 
found. Other illegalities and irregularities were found in different companies 
of Beximco Group and following those irregularities, a good number of 
recommendations were offered for reimbursement but, Beximco Group did 
not pay any heed to those recommendations. 
Accordingly, it has been  averred that on many occasions the 
companies of Beximco Group have violated laws and the respective 
circulars of Bangladesh Bank in receiving loan and as well as in repayment 
of loan including advances taken from different banks. Resultantly, it has 
produced a larger amount of classified loan in the country.  
Those assertions of  Bangladesh Bank have not been controverted by 
the respondent no.3 with supported official documents, rather, its core 
contention is based on hyper-technicality of the writ petition and locus 
standi of the petitioner.  
So far appointment of the Receiver is concerned it has been stated by 
the respondent no.1 that in compliance of the order of this Court an 
Executive Director of Bangladesh Bank has been appointed as Receiver by 
the Bangladesh Bank vide order dated 07.11.2024 (Annexure-III of the 

<<PAGE 35>>
 
35
Affidavit in Compliance dated 02.03.2025) with the respective Terms of 
Reference. 
     
However, fact remains, in the affidavit-in-compliance, filed by the 
Bangladesh Bank, neither any averment has been made nor any document 
has been produced to show the steps so far have been taken by the respective 
scheduled banks and financial institutions, who have extended loan facilities 
to the respective companies of Beximco Group, for realization of the 
defaulted loan amount, which involved huge amount of public money.  
Bangladesh Bank has also not come forward to show that it took any step 
whatsoever under the Bangladesh Bank Order, 1972 against the concerned 
banks and financial institutions for their failure to conduct their banking 
business in the interest of its/their depositors while granting loan to the 
respective companies of Beximco Group. 
The facts, as stated in the affidavit in compliance on the position of 
regular loan along with defaulted and classified amount of huge loan being 
enjoyed by the respective companies of Beximco Groups, clearly involves 
public cause. At the same time, it also manifests/exhibits the failure of 
Bangladesh Bank to discharge its respective duties and functions in its 
regulatory and supervisory capacity over the respective scheduled banks and 
financial institutions to take necessary steps in accordance with law for 
realization of the defaulted and classified loan amount. Thus, causes public 
wrong as well as public injury, for, those loan at the end of the day is public 
money being deposited by the public at large in the respective banks and 
financial institutions. Resultantly, a serious threat is being posed towards 
recovery of the said public fund.  

<<PAGE 36>>
 
36
     
 It is the categorical findings of our Appellate Division that exercise of 
discretion by the High Court Division to treat the petitioner aggrieved or not 
depends upon the facts and circumstances of each case. Accordingly, this 
Court can grant locus standi to the petitioner to maintain the writ petition for 
judicial redress of public injury arising from breach of public duty or for 
violation of the respective provisions of the Constitution or the law and seek 
enforcement of such public duty and or observance of such constitutional or 
legal provision . 
           Basing on the affidavit in compliance, in particular dated 02.03.2025 
furnished by Bangladesh Bank, there is no doubt to find that Bangladesh 
Bank as well as the respective scheduled banks and financial institutions 
have miserably failed to perform their statutory/legal obligations towards 
disbursement of loan in due compliance of law as well as realization of the 
defaulted and classified loan amount from the respective companies of 
Beximco Group. 
           In the given facts and circumstances of the case, we find that the 
petitioner has locus standi to maintain the instant writ petition filed on 
public cause involving public wrong as well as public injury. 
           No doubt, instant writ petition does not reflect strict compliance with 
the respective provisions of the Supreme Court of Bangladesh (High Court 
Division) Rules, 1973, but when an issue is brought to the judicial notice of 
this Court, the guardian of the Constitution, by filing writ petition under 
Article 102 of the Constitution involving public cause, which ultimately led 
to disclosure of public wrong or public injury, the same cannot be throttled  
down on the ground that the petition suffers from procedural defect or is not 

<<PAGE 37>>
 
37
in form. In other words, this writ petition maintains/survives in the eye of 
law considering the given facts and circumstances of the instant case. 
        Generally, question of appointment of the Receiver comes into play for 
preservation of the subject matter of the litigation pending judicial 
determination of the rights of the parties concerned.  However, in the instant 
case, the appointment of the Receiver by the respondent no.1 pursuant to the 
interim order of this Court, has not been made within the framework of the 
respective statute, rules, regulations or circulars of Bangladesh Bank. Said 
interim order has been passed by this Court considering the exigency of the 
present context of the respective companies of Beximco Group which 
involves huge  outstanding loan of Tk.53,100 crore,  sanctioned by different 
scheduled banks and financial institutions and Tk. 23,120.51 crore being 
classified as of 30.09.2024. Thus, posing significant threat to recovery of the 
said public fund. 
        Moreover, the Appellate Division while passing the order dated 
12.11.2024 in CPLA No.3174 of 2024 modifying the ad-interim order dated 
05.09.2024 passed by this Court in the instant writ petition,  did not interfere 
with the interim  order of appointment of the Receiver and attachment of the 
properties of the respective companies of Beximco Group save and except 
respondent no.3. 
             In view of the facts and circumstances of the case, appointing a 
receiver by Bangladesh Bank  pursuant to the interim order passed by this 
Court and having not been  interfered with by the Hon’ble Appellate 
Division, is found lawful under the doctrine of necessity as embodied in the 
maxim, salus populi est suprema lex (public welfare is the hightest law).  

<<PAGE 38>>
 
38
Consequently, all acts, steps, decisions taken by the said Receiver in 
good faith, to protect public fund being disbursed to the respective 
companies of Beximco Group by allowing loan beyond the sanctioned limit 
including the loan which has now, become classified, also the loan amount 
which is at the verge of being classified and to protect, among others, 
unlawful transfer of properties or assets of those companies, are mandated as 
lawful for the greater interest and welfare of the people concerned.  
      The technical defect, however, being raised on behalf of the respondent 
no.3 as to filing vokatnama on behalf of the respondent no.1 subsequent to 
filing the affidavits in compliance, is cured with the filing of affidavit in 
compliance dated 02.03.2025, which is subsequent to filing power, 
containing all the informations and steps so far have been taken by  
Bangladesh Bank in compliance of the interim direction given by this Court 
along with necessary documents. 
Considering facts and circumstances, observations and findings of this 
Court following decisions have been taken: 
1. The petitioner has locus standi to maintain the instant writ petition  
on public cause involving public wrong and public injury; 
2. Basing on the affidavit in compliance furnished by the respondent 
no.1 Bangladesh Bank dated 02.03.2025 there is no doubt to find 
that Bangladesh Bank as well its respective scheduled banks and 
financial institutions have miserably failed to perform and or 
observe their statutory or legal obligations towards disbursement 
of loan as well as realization of the defaulted and classified loan 
amount from the respective companies of Beximco Group, the 

<<PAGE 39>>
 
39
consequence of which shall ultimately be borne by the public in 
general; 
3. When an issue, involving public cause leading to disclosure 
through records of public wrong or public injury, has been 
brought to the judicial notice of this Court, the door to justice 
cannot be closed on the ground that the respective petition suffers 
from procedural defect/ or is not in form; 
4. The appointment of the Receiver in the instant case has not been 
made within the framework of the respective statues, rules, 
regulations or circulars of Bangladesh Bank, but considering the 
exigency of the present context of Beximco Group  of Companies, 
involving outstanding loan of Tk.53,000 crore and Tk.23,120.51 
crore being classified as of 30.09.2024.  
In the given facts and circumstances, appointing a receiver by 
the respondent no.1, Bangladesh Bank pursuant to the interim 
order passed by this Court and having not been interfered with by 
the Hon’ble Appellate Division, except respondent No.3, is found 
lawful under the doctrine of necessity as embodied in the maxim, 
salus populi est suprema lex (public welfare is the highest law). 
Consequently, all acts, steps, decisions taken by the said 
Receiver in good faith to protect the public fund being disbursed to 
the respective companies of Beximco Group by allowing loan 
beyond the sanctioned limit, including the loan which has now 
become classified; also, the loan amount which is at the verge of 
being classified and to protect, among others, unlawful transfer of 

<<PAGE 40>>
 
40
properties and assets of those companies, are mandated as lawful 
for the greater interest and welfare of the people concern; 
5.   All actions, steps, decisions taken by the respondent no.1,     
Bangladesh Bank and the Government of Bangladesh pursuant to 
the interim order passed by this Court in connection with the 
instant writ petition are found lawful for the safeguard of public 
money;  
6.  The conducts of Bangladesh Bank, respondent no. 1 and all 
concerned scheduled banks and financial institutions towards 
sanctioning loan facilities to Beximco Group of Companies 
violating banking rules and regulations have become questionable;  
In the given context, respondent no. 1 and other concerned 
authorities are hereby directed to take necessary steps against the 
person(s) and/or institution(s) concerned, who are responsible for 
sanctioning said questionable loan facilities to the respective 
companies of Beximco Group of Companies, in accordance with 
law;  
7. Bangladesh Bank, respondent no.1 as being the regulatory and 
supervisory authority over the respective scheduled banks and 
financial institutions, is hereby directed to take all necessary steps 
towards realization of the defaulted and classified loan amount of 
Beximco Group of Companies within the bound of law;  
8. Bangladesh Bank, respondent no.1, Bangladesh Financial 
Intelligence Unit, respondent no.7 and other concerned authorities of 
the government are hereby directed to complete the process of 
investigation/enquiry, if any, so has/have been initiated on the issue in 

<<PAGE 41>>
 
41
question and on completion thereof, if found substance, to take 
necessary steps in accordance with law; and  
9. Considering the interest of thousands of workers, staffs and 
employees, Beximco Group of Companies is allowed to run the 
management and affairs of its respective companies in strict 
compliance of law under the constant monitoring of the concerned 
departments of the Government of Bangladesh including Bangladesh 
Bank and other concerned statutory bodies. 
In view of the above directions, the interim order of appointment of 
the Receiver and attachment of properties of Beximco Group of Companies 
being redundant is hereby vacated.  
With the above, this Rule is accordingly disposed of without any order 
as to costs.  
Communicate the judgment and order to the respondents concerned at 
once. 
 
