","The PETITIONER, a lawyer appearing in person, sought public-interest directions to the central-bank RESPONDENT to disclose loan information of a corporate group, appoint a receiver, attach its assets and recover defaulted public funds. He relied on Article 102 and Chapter XIA of the High Court Division Rules, 1973, alleging regulatory inaction and concealed beneficial ownership. The corporate RESPONDENT denied default and argued lack of locus standi, procedural defects and absence of statutory power to appoint a receiver. The central-bank and financial-intelligence RESPONDENTS relied on compliance affidavits, the Bank Companies Act, 1991, the Bangladesh Bank Order, 1972 and money-laundering rules. The dispute concerns maintainability, protection of depositors’ money and lawful recovery measures.","Constitution of Bangladesh, Article 102",PARTLY,"UNCERTAIN: the judgment invokes Article 102, banking statutes and the doctrine of necessity. The court held that the PETITIONER had sufficient interest because the classified loans involved public money and regulatory failure. Procedural defects did not defeat a public cause once official records disclosed public injury. The central bank and lenders had failed statutory duties in sanctioning and recovering loans. The receiver lacked a statutory framework, but necessity validated protective steps. Recovery, accountability and investigation directions were issued, while receivership and attachment ended and monitored management continued.",79e6678791ddc8bb8d7cc150b474688808d99092e7135aedc132562f358850a4
