","The PETITIONER challenged a Land Survey Tribunal decree dated 23.06.2019 under Article 102, contending that the Land Survey Appellate Tribunal had not been established when the writ was filed and that no other efficacious appellate remedy was available. During the pendency of the Rule, the STATE constituted the appellate tribunal, but the statutory period for filing an appeal expired while the writ remained pending. The PETITIONER sought relief from the decree and an opportunity to appeal. The STATE RESPONDENTS were heard. The dispute concerns the proper forum for factual issues arising from the decree and the procedural consequences of the appellate tribunal’s delayed establishment.",Not stated,REFUSED,"UNCERTAIN: the Rule was disposed of without entering into the merits, so the challenge to the decree was not substantively decided. The court found that the factual issues were suitable for the Land Survey Appellate Tribunal, which had since been established. Following earlier benches, it considered that justice required permitting an appeal despite the expired limitation period. It directed admission of an appeal filed within ninety days, maintained interim protection until filing and required status quo, while leaving the decree itself unexamined.",2346e512a5f1b78f0ee94e938b17b5fec97d93de6930ed541eb6ce93b166cd6c
