Md. Mozibur Rahman Miah, J. 
On an application under article 102 of the Constitution of the 
People’s Republic of Bangladesh, a Rule Nisi was issued calling upon the 
respondents 
to 
show 
cause 
as 
to 
why 
the 
Memo 
No. 
05.44.4700.031.32.569.17-462(1) dated 22.02.2017 issued by the 
respondent no. 1 as evident by Annexure-F shall not be declared to have 

<<PAGE 2>>
 
2 
been issued without lawful authority and to be of no legal effect and/or 
such other or further order or orders passed as to this Court may seem fit 
and proper. 
At the time of issuance of the rule, the operation of the impugned 
memo issued by the respondent no. 1 was stayed for a period of 06(six) 
months. On the same date, this court also passed an order restraining the 
respondents by an order of injunction from disturbing peaceful possession 
of the petitioner in the disputed land for a period of 06 (six) months. That 
interim order of stay was subsequently extended from time to time and it 
was lastly extended on 27.07.2025 for another 06 (six) months.  
The salient facts leading to issuance of the instant rule are: 
The present respondent no. 2, Bangladesh House Building Finance  
Corporation filed a Miscellaneous Case under Article 27 of Presidential 
Order no. 7 of 1973 (PO 7 of 1973) against the present petitioner and 
another for recovery of defaulted loan amounting to taka 5,27,881/30. It 
has been stated in the petition  filed under Article 27 of PO 7 of 1973 that 
gave rise to Miscellaneous Case No. 69 of 1999 that the respondent no. 2 
sanctiond an amount of taka 1,44,000/- through Rin (loan) Case No. BK-
2551 at a interest rate of taka 11% per annum fixing repayment at taka 
1277.15 as of monthly installment. Since the petitioner failed to repay the 
installment as per the sanction letter, the said respondent then initiated 
that Miscellaneous Case.  
The learned District Judge, Khulna then took up the said Case for 
hearing and after taking into consideration of the materials and evidence 
on record, vide judgment and decree dated 16.06.2004 allowed the said 

<<PAGE 3>>
 
3 
Miscellaneous Case. As the opposite parties to the Miscellaneous Case 
did not come forward to pay back the decreetal amount, the respondent no. 
2 then initiated an execution case being Title Execution Case No. 42 of 
2004. In course of the said execution case the property so have been 
mortgaged with the respondent no. 2 then put on auction sale to realize the 
decreetal dues and ultimately it was sold out in a thorough away price, the 
petitioner then  filed a Miscellaneous Case under order 21 rule 90 of the 
Code of Civil Procedure for setting aside the said auction alleging that no 
summons or notice was served upon them (judgment debtors). However, 
without following the provision so have been provided in Order 21 rule 
69(2) and rules 66,67,85 and 86 of the Code of Civil Procedure, the 
executing court proceed with the auction sale and sold out the mortgaged 
property in favour of the auction purchaser and as such the said auction is 
liable to be set aside. It has also been asserted in the said Miscellaneous 
Case that, though meantime the petitioner repaid an amount of taka 
8,80,000/- to the respondent no. 2 yet that very vital aspect has not been 
taken into consideration. In the said Miscellaneous Case, the petitioner 
examined himself as PW-1 while respondent no. 2 also examined 1 
witness. Apart from that both the parties also produced several documents 
in support of their respective claimed which were marked as exhibits. 
However, the learned District Judge without considering the materials and 
evidence on record ultimately dismissed the said Miscellaneous Case vide 
judgment and order dated 06.08.2015.  
Feeling aggrieved by and dissatisfied with the said order dated 
06.08.2015 the petitioner and his brother who are the judgment debtors in  

<<PAGE 4>>
 
4 
the execution case as appellants then preferred an appeal being First 
Miscellaneous Appeal being Tender No. 462 of 2016 before this court 
where they also filed an application for stay of the operation of the 
impugned judgment passed in the said Miscellaneous Case on which this 
court also passed an order of status quo which gave rise to Civil Rule No. 
791(FM) of 2016. However, when the said First Miscellaneous Appeal as 
well as the rule was pending, respondent no. 1 all of a sudden, issued a  
notice 
upon 
the 
petitioner 
vide 
impugned 
memo 
no. 
05.44.4700.031.32.569.17-462(1) dated 22.02.2017  giving 15 days time 
to hand over possession of the disputed building to the auction 
purchaser/respondent no. 2.  
It is at that stage, the judgment debtor no. 2 as petitioner came 
before this court and obtained instant rule and order of stay and injunction 
as stated above.  
Ms. Syeda Shoukat Ara, the learned counsel appearing for the 
petitioner upon taking us to the writ petition and all the documents 
appended therewith, at the very outset submits that, the impugned notice 
has not disclosed under what provision of law, the respondent no. 1 has 
issued the impugned notice which is illegal, illogical and vague and the 
same is liable to be declared to have been issued without lawful authority 
and is of no legal effect.  
The learned counsel goes on to submit that, PO No. 7 of 1973 has 
not been mandated a Deputy Commissioner to issue such a notice either in 
a proceeding filed and disposed of in a Miscellaneous Case or an 
execution case initiated under Article 27 of PO 7 of 1973 or under order 

<<PAGE 5>>
 
5 
21 rule 90 of the Code of Civil Procedure respectively so the impugned 
notice issued by respondent no. 1 is without lawful authority and is of no 
legal effect.  
It has also been asserted by the learned counsel that the petitioner 
has still been possessing the suit building by paying holding tax and 
utility bills to the respective department of the government. It has also 
been asserted by the learned counsel that, against the impugned action 
taken by the respondent no. 1 since there has been no other alternative 
efficacious remedy, the petitioner has thus compelled to file the instant 
writ petition and obtained rule and order of stay and thus the impugned 
letter is liable to be set aside by making the rule absolute.  
On the contrary, Mr. Mohammad Saiful Islam, the learned counsel 
appearing for the respondent no. 2 though opposed the contention taken 
by the learned counsel for the petitioner but frankly submits that at the 
time of issuance of the impugned notice by the respondent no. 1, the 
respondent no. 2 has got no knowledge about the interim order passed by 
this Hon’ble court vide Civil Rule No. 791(FM) of 2016 and if the 
respondent no. 2 had prior knowledge about filing of the FMA as well as 
rule and the interim order passed thereof, it would not have asked the 
Deputy Commissioner, respondent no. 1 to issue the impugned notice.  
The learned counsel lastly contends that, though the First 
Miscellaneous Appeal and the rule is still pending so in the light of the 
above submission the rule may kindly be disposed of.  
We have considered the submission so placed by the learned 
counsel for the petitioner and that of the learned counsel for the 

<<PAGE 6>>
 
6 
respondent no. 2. We have very meticulously gone through the impugned 
letter so have been issued by the respondent no. 1 asking the petitioner to 
hand over possession of the disputed building. On going through the writ 
petition as well as the annexure, we find that, at the instance of the 
petitioner, First Miscellaneous Appeal was preferred and in the said 
Appeal, the petitioner also filed an application for stay and injunction and 
this court vide order dated 02.11.2016 issued rule as well as restrained the 
respondents from disturbing the peaceful possession of the petitioner in 
the suit land for a period of 06 (six) months. But from Annexure-‘F’ to the 
writ petition issued by respondent no. 1 we find that during subsistence of 
that interim order by this court he issued the impugned notice on 
22.02.2017 who has got no authority to issue such kind of letter. 
Furthermore, the respondent no. 1, the Deputy Commissioner is so callous 
that he did not feel it necessary to comprehend under what authority he 
was going to issue the impugned notice.   
Regardless, we don’t find any shred of merit or substance in issuing 
such kind vexatious notice keeping pendency of rule an appeal and where 
a restrained order on possession of the suit property is in place.  
As a result, the rule is made absolute however without any order as 
to costs.  
The impugned Memo No. 05.44.4700.031.32.569.17-462(1) dated 
22.02.2017 issued by the respondent no. 1 (Annexure-‘F’ to the writ 
petition) thus stands cancelled.  
In any case, the order of stay and injunction granted at the time of 
issuance of the rule stands recalled and vacated.  

