","The PETITIONER, a judgment debtor, challenged a Deputy Commissioner’s memo dated 22.02.2017 requiring him to hand over a mortgaged building to an auction-purchaser corporation. The dispute arose from a loan-recovery decree under Article 27 of Presidential Order No. 7 of 1973 and an execution auction. The PETITIONER had unsuccessfully objected to the sale under Order XXI rule 90 of the Code of Civil Procedure and had a pending First Miscellaneous Appeal with an interim possession-protection order. He argued that the memo lacked legal authority and that the Presidential Order did not empower the Deputy Commissioner to issue it. The corporation RESPONDENT said it had been unaware of the interim order and noted that the appeal remained pending.",Not stated,ALLOWED,"The court found that the Deputy Commissioner issued the possession-handover notice while a High Court interim order in the pending First Miscellaneous Appeal restrained disturbance of the PETITIONER’S possession. It held that the officer had no authority to issue such a notice and had failed to consider the legal basis for doing so. The RESPONDENT’S lack of knowledge of the interim order did not validate the notice. Because the notice was vexatious and inconsistent with the subsisting protective order, the court cancelled it and made the Rule absolute.",a684ead635cf728484cbf39014513aabf3cf72c1bde4fca49ad99396046e0485
