This rule was issued calling upon the opposite party nos. 1-5 to 
show cause as to why the judgment and order dated 15.07.2024 passed 
by the learned District Judge, Noakhali in Miscellaneous Case No. 35 
of 2024 allowing the petitioner’s application filed under Section 24 of 
the Code of Civil Procedure transferring  Land Survey Tribunal Suit 
No. 502 of 2014 and Title Suit No. 320 of 2010 pending in the Court 
of Begumgonj Senior Assistant Judge, Noakhali to the Assistant Judge 

<<PAGE 2>>
 
2
Court, Chatkhil, Noakhali for being heard analogously and 
simultaneously with Title Suit No. 427 of 2021, 422 of 2021 and 426 
of 2021 pending in the Court of Assistant Judge, Chatkhil, Noakhali 
should not be set aside and/or such other or further order or orders be 
passed as to this Court may seem fit and proper.  
At the time of issuance of the rule, the operation of the order 
dated 15.07.2024 passed by the learned District Judge, Noakhali in 
Miscellaneous Case No. 35 of 2024 was stayed for a period of 06(six) 
months, which was lastly extended on 19.05.2025 for another period 
of 06(six) months. 
Facts leading to the issuance of the Rule, in brief, are that one 
Amarendro Narayan Bosak @ Amar Mitro and four others filed 
Miscellaneous case No. 35 of 2024 under Section 24 of The Civil 
Procedure Code praying for transfer of Land Survey Tribunal (L.S.T.) 
Suit No. 502 of 2010 pending in the Tribunal and Title Suit No. 320 
of  2010 pending in the Assistant Judge Court, Chatkhil, Noakhali for 
being heard analogously and simultaneously with Title Suit No. 427 
of 2021, 422 of 2021 and 426 of 2021 pending in that Court. The case 
of the petitioners is that the parties in all these suits are substantially 
the same and are contesting several civil suits in different courts 
concerning land recorded under MRR Khatian Nos. 104 and 154 and 
District Survey Khatian Nos. 116 and 169 under Mouza Mokilla, 

<<PAGE 3>>
 
3
Police Station Begumgonj, District Noakhali. The petitioners as 
plaintiffs instituted Title Suit No. 427 of 2021 for declaration of title 
against the opposite party nos. 1-27 which is pending before the 
learned Assistant Judge, Chatkhil and was fixed on 30.09.2024 for 
final hearing. Subsequently, petitioner nos. 4 and 5 as plaintiff 
instituted Title Suit no. 422 of 2021 for declaration of title against 
petitioner nos. 1, 2 and 3 and opposite party nos. 1-27 which was 
fixed on 30.09.2024 for framing of issues. Another Partition Suit no. 
426 of 2021 was filed before the same Court, against petitioner nos. 1-
4 and also fixed on 30.09.2024 for framing of issues. On the other 
hand, the opposite party as plaintiff instituted Title Suit No. 320 of 
2010 for declaration of title in respect of the same property which is 
pending before the learned Senior Assistant Judge Court, Begumgonj, 
Noakhali, and was fixed on 11.08.2024 for S.D. Another case, Land 
Survey Tribunal (LST) Suit no. 502 of 2014 was filed by the opposite 
party no. 7 as plaintiff wherein the petitioner nos. 36 made as 
defendant alongwith opposite party nos. 1-4 which is pending before 
the learned Senior Assistant Judge Court, Begumgonj, Noakhali  and 
was fixed on 28.07.2024 for trial.  
It is contended that the above-mentioned suits involve the same 
land and documents and are under trial in different courts of  
Noakhali. Consequently, there is a risk of multiplicity of proceedings, 

<<PAGE 4>>
 
4
harassment of parties, increased costs, and conflicting judgments, 
which may lead to further complexity and obstruction of justice. 
Therefore, it was prayed that all the suits be tried analogously in one 
court in the interest of justice. 
Upon hearing, the learned District Judge, Noakhali allowed the 
application under Section 24 of the Civil Procedure Code and 
transferred the LST Suit No. 502 of 2014 pending in the Tribunal and 
Title Suit No. 320 of 2010 pending in the Court of Begumgonj Senior 
Assistant Judge to the Court of the Assistant Judge, Chatkhil, 
Noakhali for being heard analogously and simultaneously with Title 
Suit No. 427 of 2021, 422 of 2021 and 426 of 2021. 
Being aggrieved by and dissatisfied with the judgment and order 
dated 15.07.2024 passed by the learned District Judge, Noakhali the 
petitioner preferred this Civil Revision and obtained Rule and order of 
stay. 
Mr. Md. Azahar Ullah Bhuiyan, the learned Advocate for the 
petitioner submits that the opposite parties, by suppressing material 
facts filed and moved Miscellaneous Case No. 35 of 2024 under 
Section 24 of the Code of Civil Procedure for transferring LST Suit 
No. 502 of 2014 pending before Tribunal along with Title Suit No. 
320 of 2010 pending before the learned District Judge Court, Noakhali 

<<PAGE 5>>
 
5
on 15.07.2024 and obtained ex parte order on the same day by 
practicing fraud upon the said Court. 
He next submits that the Ministry of Law, Justice and 
Parliamentary Affairs, Law and Justice Division, Justice Section-4 by 
Memo 
No. 
10.00.0000.128.15.001.20-478, 
dated 
01.11.2023 
empowered Senior Assistant Judge and Assistant Judges to act as 
Additional Land Survey Tribunal in respect of LST suits for early 
disposal of the suits and authorized the Land Survey Tribunal to 
transfer the pending Case accordingly. Pursuant to this LST Suit No. 
502 of 2014 was transferred to the Senior Assistant Judge Court, 
Begumgonj and Land Survey Tribunal, Noakhali (Additional). The 
opposite parties neither sought any transfer of the said LST Suit nor 
challenged the said transfer order dated 06.11.2023 before the Land 
Survey Appellate Tribunal. 
Mr.  Bhuiyan further contends that under the law, the learned District 
Judge has no jurisdiction to interfere with the judgment or order of the 
Land Survey Tribunal, as the Government has established separate 
Land Appellate Tribunals for such matters. Therefore, the learned 
District Judge, Noakhali acted without lawful authority in entertaining 
and allowing Miscellaneous Case No. 35 of 2024 and transferring 
L.S.T. Suit No. 502 of 2014. 

<<PAGE 6>>
 
6
The learned Counsel also contends that the Miscellaneous Case was 
filed with an application seeking dispensation of Service of Notice 
upon the respondents Nos. 5, 6, 8, 11, 13, 17, 19-21, 25-37, 39-94, 
101-106 and 112 marking them as 'Non- Appearing and Non-
Contesting' although the petitioner is the plaintiff  in the Land Survey 
Tribunal Suit and is actively contesting all the suits including Title 
Suit No. 320 of 2010.  Hence, the learned District Judge erred in 
allowing the Miscellaneous Case ex parte and transferring the cases as 
prayed for. With those submissions the learned counsel finally prays 
for making the Rule absolute. 
Despite service, no one appears on behalf of the opposite parties 
to oppose the Rule.  
I have heard the learned Advocate for the petitioner, perused the 
revisional application, the impugned order and other materials on 
record.  
It appears from the record that Land Survey Tribunal Suit 
No.502 of 2014 is pending before the Senior Assistant Judge Court, 
Begumgonj and Land Survey Tribunal, Noakhali (Additional). 
Nevertheless, the learned District Judge, Noakhali transferred the suit 
to Assistant Judge Court, Chatkhil, Noakhali for being heard 
analogously and simultaneously with other civil suits. Such transfer is 
patently without jurisdiction as a Land Survey Tribunal Suit cannot be 
transferred to a civil court created under the Civil Courts Act. 

<<PAGE 7>>
 
7
A Land Survey Tribunal is constituted under the special provisions of 
the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950 for adjudicating disputes 
relating to the correctness of the last revised record of rights and 
ensuring accuracy in land records. The transferee Court cannot hear a 
Land Survey Tribunal Suit with ordinary civil suits analogously or 
simultaneously because transferee Court has no jurisdiction to hear the 
matter relating to LST suit and the jurisdiction of Land Survey 
Tribunal is limited and exclusive to adjudicating disputes arising from 
the final publication of the record-of-rights, and civil courts are 
expressly barred from hearing these matters. Matters that are not 
related to the record-of-rights must be handled by a civil court, but 
any case that is a ""suit arising out of the final publication of the last 
revised record-of-rights"" will be transferred to the Tribunal, which has 
exclusive jurisdiction over those cases under Section 145 of State 
Acquisition and Tenancy Act. Once a case falls under the Tribunal's 
jurisdiction, civil courts cannot hear that specific matter. If a civil 
court already has a case arising from the final record-of-rights, it will 
be transferred to the Land Survey Tribunal once it is established. 
Other disputes on title that are not connected to the final record-of-
rights can be heard by civil courts, while the Land Survey Tribunal 
handles its specific mandate. 
The land Survey Tribunal is not a civil court subordinate to the 
District Judge and hence, the learned District Judge, Noakhali has no 

<<PAGE 8>>
 
8
authority to transfer such a suit under section 24 of the Code of Civil 
Procedure. 
Given the facts and circumstances of the case, I do not find any 
shred of substance in the impugned order which is liable to be set 
aside. I am of the view that the Rule succeeds on merit. 
In the result, the Rule is made absolute, however, without any 
order as to costs. 
The impugned judgment and order dated 15.07.2024 passed by 
the learned District Judge; Noakhali in Miscellaneous Case No. 35 of 
2024 is hereby set aside.  
The order of stay granted earlier by this court at the time of 
issuance of the Rule stands recalled and vacated. 
Let a copy of this Judgment be communicated to the concerned 
Court forthwith. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
","The PETITIONER challenged a District Judge’s order under section 24 of the Code of Civil Procedure transferring a Land Survey Tribunal suit and a title suit to an Assistant Judge for analogous hearing with other civil suits. The OPPOSITE PARTIES had sought the transfer to avoid multiplicity, conflicting decisions and harassment. The PETITIONER argued that the Land Survey Tribunal suit had already been assigned to an Additional Land Survey Tribunal, that the District Judge lacked jurisdiction over it, and that the transfer order was obtained ex parte by suppressing material facts and misclassifying contesting parties. The revision concerns the boundary between ordinary civil jurisdiction and the exclusive record-of-rights jurisdiction of the Land Survey Tribunal.",Other: State Acquisition and Tenancy Act 1950,ALLOWED,"The court held that a Land Survey Tribunal is constituted under the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950 and has exclusive jurisdiction over suits arising from the final publication of the last revised record-of-rights. It is not a civil court subordinate to the District Judge, and section 24 of the Code of Civil Procedure does not authorize transfer of such a suit to an ordinary civil court. Because the transferee court lacked jurisdiction to hear the Tribunal suit analogously with title suits, the transfer order was patently without jurisdiction and could not stand.",8698cfa7546c8ac4ed2ebd8dcc333c0e7fb86a4bc7538f60843007ffebebe082
