","The PETITIONER, a third-party purchaser, sought addition as a RESPONDENT in a partition appeal and allotment of four decimals from the share decreed to his vendors’ predecessors. He claimed a registered purchase during the appeal from a vendor who had bought during the suit, followed by mutation, rent payment and possession within definite boundaries. The plaintiff-OPPOSITE PARTIES opposed addition, arguing that the transfers were pendente lite, that the PETITIONER lacked ejmali possession and that he was not a necessary party. The dispute concerns whether a purchaser with a separately bounded plot must join the appeal to protect his claim or must look to the share allotted to his vendors.",Transfer of Property Act 1882,REFUSED,"The court found that both transfers occurred while the partition litigation was pending and that the PETITIONER claimed a specifically bounded plot in possession, not an undivided ejmali interest requiring allotment in the appeal. The plaintiff-OPPOSITE PARTIES had not challenged the share decreed to the vendor-OPPOSITE PARTIES from whom the PETITIONER’S title derived, and those vendor-OPPOSITE PARTIES had not appealed. The PETITIONER could therefore realize his purchased land from that allotted share. He was not a necessary or proper party, and the appellate court’s refusal to add him was correct.",d08604cb6ddd518ef6a72453bdb58454c6091a44ce37bac495de591001830f38
