instant writ petition and obtained rule and order of stay.  
Mr. Moni Shankar Sarker, the learned counsel appearing for the 
petitioner upon taking us to the writ petition and by reading out two 
judgments of the courts below  at the very outset submits that the learned 
judge of the Appellate tribunal below committed an error of law in not 

<<PAGE 7>>
 
7 
taking into consideration of the material fact that the predecessor of the 
plaintiffs in vested property case no. 418 of 2012 left the country during 
India-Pakistan War in 1965 having no scope to get the property by way of 
purchase by the respondent nos. 3 and 4 as plaintiffs. 
The learned counsel by taking us to the grounds to the writ petition 
also contends that the present petitioner got the property by way of lease 
vide settlement case no. 68 of 1981 and 1982 and the petitioner has still 
been possessing the suit property having no scope for the respondent nos. 
3 and 4 to get a decree in their favour by releasing the same from ‘Ka’ 
schedule. When we pose a question to the learned counsel for the 
petitioner what is the locustandi of the petitioner to file a writ petition 
claiming to be lessee the learned counsel then finds it difficult to give any 
plausible reply to our said query though finally prays for making the rule 
absolute on setting aside the impugned judgment and decree. 
On the contrary, Mr. Tapas Bandhu Das, the learned counsel 
appearing for the respondent nos.  4 and 5 vehemently opposes the 
contention taken by the learned counsel for the petitioner and contends 
that, the petitioner has got no locustandi to file the instant writ petition 
since under the relevant provision provided in Aripta Sampatti Prottarpan 
Ain, 2001 as a lessee can not be any party to a suit or can challenge the 
decree so passed by the tribunal as well as the appellate tribunal. In 
support of his such submission, the learned counsel has then placed his 
reliance in the decision reported in 56 DLR (AD) 72 and take us to 
paragraph no. 17 thereof and submits that in that decision it is held that 
even before enacting Aripta Sampatti Prottarpan Ain, 2001 it has been 

<<PAGE 8>>
 
8 
settled therein that a lessee has got no locustandi to add or contest any suit 
challenging enlistment of any property as vested property and the said 
decision is equally applicable in the facts and circumstances of the instant 
case. 
The learned counsel further contends that, since the respondent nos. 
4 and 5 have been able to prove their case in toto by adducing and 
producing sufficient material documents, the learned judge of both the 
courts below on due considering of those materials on record correctly 
decreed the suit.  
The learned counsel also submits that the appellate tribunal below 
has rightly modified the judgment and decree of the tribunal since the 
proforma defendant nos. 2 and 3 did not pray for releasing their respective 
property enlisted in ‘Ka’ schedule as per the provision of section 5 of the 
Aripta Sampatti Prottarpan Ain, 2001 and for that obvious reason the 
respondents have not challenged the judgment passed by the appellate 
tribunal.  
The learned counsel lastly contends that, since all the document 
produced by the respondent nos. 4 and 5 as plaintiffs were marked as 
exhibits without any objection by the respondent no. 3 so the enlistment 
of the property in ‘Ka’ scheduled under section 9(1) of the Aripta 
Sampatti Prottarpan Ain, 2001 is totally illegal and finally prays for 
discharging the rule.  
Though in the instant writ petition, the government represented by 
the Deputy Commissioner has been made as respondent no. 3 but the 
learned Deputy Attorney General submits that since he did not get any 

<<PAGE 9>>
 
9 
instruction for taking any step in regard to the instant writ petition, so he 
is unable to make any submission to that effect.  
Be that as it may, we have considered the submission so placed by 
the learned counsel for the petitioner and that of the respondent nos. 4 and 
5 and very meticulously gone through the judgment and decree under 
challenge in this writ petition and all the documents appended therewith. 
Since the learned counsel for the respondent nos. 4 and 5 in his 
submission raised the question of maintainability of the writ petition so 
we are not inclined to dwell on other factual aspects raised in the instant 
writ petition. However, it has consistently been settled by this court as 
well as the Appellate Division, that a lessee from the government has no 
locustandi to add any party in a suit as well as in an appeal  challenging 
the propriety of enlisted any property as vested property. So under no 
circumstances can the writ petition be maintained. Apart from that, on 
careful perusal of the judgment passed by the Orpitta Shampatti (A¢fÑa)   
prattapan tribunal as well as appellate tribunal we find that both the courts 
passed the judgment by discussing evidences exhaustively allowing the 
case of the respondent nos. 4-5 by releasing their purchased property 
measuring 78 decimals of land from ‘Ka’ schedule published in the 
gazette dated 15.04.2012 made under serial no. 313. Since we don’t find 
any material irregularity or  illegality in both the judgments passed by the 
tribunal as well as appellate tribunal, we thus refrain ourselves from 
discussing or making any adverse observation with regard to the merit of 
the case since in essence, it is a judgment of affirmance.  

<<PAGE 10>>
 
10 
Regard being had to the above facts and circumstances, we don’t 
find any shred of merit in the instant rule.  
 
Accordingly, the rule is discharged however without any order as to 
costs.  
 
The judgment and decree dated 25.09.2016 passed by the learned 
judge, Aripta Sampatti Prottarpan, Additional Appellate Tribunal No. 5, 
Sylhet in Arpita Appeal No. 12 of 2015 is thus affirmed.  
 
The respondent no. 3 is hereby directed to take necessary step in 
releasing 78 decimals of land from scheduled ‘Ka’ so have been published 
in the gazette dated 15.04.2012 serialized as serial no. 313 as per the 
judgment and decree dated 25.09.2016 passed in Aripta Appeal No. 12 of 
2015 by the respondent no. 1 within 1(one) month from the date of receipt 
of the copy of this judgment and order.  
The order of stay and status quo granted at the time of issuance of 
the rule stands recalled and vacated.  
 
Let a copy of this judgment and order be communicated to the 
respondent nos. 1-3 forthwith.    
 
Rezaul Karim, J.    
 
 
 
 
 I agree. 
 
 
Kawsar/A.B.O.  
","The PETITIONER, claiming as a government lessee in possession under a vested-property settlement, challenged an appellate tribunal decree that modified a restoration decree by releasing 78 decimals out of 1.56 acres from the “Ka” schedule. He argued that the original owners had left the country during the 1965 war, that the property had validly vested, and that the plaintiff-RESPONDENTS could not acquire it by purchase. The plaintiff-RESPONDENTS relied on registered deeds, mutation, rent payment and an objection-case order, and contended that a lessee had no locus standi under the Arpita Sampatti Prottarpan Ain, 2001. The dispute concerns the maintainability of the writ and the validity of the partial release from vested-property enlistment.",Other: Arpita Sampatti Prottarpan Ain 2001,REFUSED,"The court held that a government lessee has no locus standi to be added as a party or to challenge a decree concerning enlistment of property as vested property, so the writ was not maintainable. It also found that the tribunal and appellate tribunal had exhaustively considered the evidence and that no material irregularity or illegality appeared in the partial release of the purchased land. Because the challenge failed at the threshold and the decrees were otherwise sound, the court affirmed the appellate decree and directed implementation of the release.",133c083db9a9e5613d106a62f58d887838adf6d99d27040fb270872087230373
