IN THE SUPREME COURT OF BANGLADESH 
HIGH COURT DIVISION 
(CIVIL REVISIONAL JURISDICTION) 
 
Present: 
Mr. Justice Md. Toufiq Inam 
Civil Revision No. 4302 of 2012. 
Begum Khaja Eliza Yeasmin and another. 
 ----Defendant Nos. 2, 4 & 5/Respondents-Petitioners. 
                     -Versus- 
Howa Begum and others. 
         ---- Plaintiffs-Appellants-Opposite Parties. 
 
Mr. M. F. Ahmed, Advocates 
----For the Defendant Nos. 2, 4 & 5/Respondents- 
                                                              Petitioners. 
Mr. Ali Ahsan Mullah, Advocate 
     ----For the Plaintiffs-Appellants-Opposite Parties. 
 
Heard On: 27.10.2025, 28.10.2025. 
                       And 
Date of Judgment: 30th  Day of September 2025. 
 
Md. Toufiq Inam, J. 
This Rule was issued calling upon the opposite parties to show cause 
as to why the judgment and decree dated 25.09.2012 (decree signed 
on 01.10.2012) passed by the learned Joint District Judge, 3rd Court, 
Sylhet, in Title Appeal No. 144 of 2008, reversing the judgment and 
decree dated 27.10.2008 (decree signed on 03.11.2008) passed by the 
learned Senior Assistant Judge, Sadar, Sylhet, in Title Suit No. 166 of 
2000 (renumbered as Title Suit No. 49 of 2004), should not be set 

<<PAGE 2>>
2 
 
aside or such other order passed as to this Court may seem fit and 
proper. 
 
The facts, in brief, are that the plaintiffs instituted Title Suit No. 166 
of 2000 on 10.07.2000 for cancellation of registered sale deed No. 
14477 dated 27.10.1992 alleging that the said deed was forged and 
fabricated, executed in the name of one Sajina Bibi who had allegedly 
died prior to its execution. The suit land described in Schedule ―Ka‖ 
of the plaint pertains to S.A. Khatian Nos. 532 and 534 of Mouza–
Hazipur, P.S.–Sadar, District–Sylhet. 
 
According to the plaintiffs, Sajina Bibi died on 26.06.1991 leaving 
them as her heirs. After her death, they continued in possession of the 
land. However, in 2000, they allegedly came to know that the 
defendants were claiming title on the basis of a sale deed purportedly 
executed by Sajina Bibi. Upon obtaining a certified copy of the deed 
on 12.07.2000, they found that it was executed in 1992—after her 
death—and thus filed the instant suit seeking its cancellation. 
 
The contesting defendants denied all allegations and asserted that the 
suit was not maintainable, barred by limitation, and bad for defect of 
parties. They contended that Sajina Bibi was alive on 27.10.1992 and 
voluntarily executed the deed for valuable consideration in their 

<<PAGE 3>>
3 
 
favour. They further claimed to have taken possession immediately 
upon purchase and have remained in possession since, by constructing 
homesteads and a pond. They asserted that Sajina Bibi died later, on 
02.10.1993. 
 
The plaintiffs examined three witnesses (PW-1 to PW-3) and 
produced three exhibits including the impugned deed and an alleged 
death certificate (Exhibit–3). PW-3, the Imam who supposedly issued 
the certificate, denied having issued or signed such document. The 
plaintiffs failed to produce any attesting witness of the deed or to have 
the thumb impression examined by an expert. 
 
The defendants examined four witnesses including one attesting 
witness (DW-2), who deposed that he was present at the execution 
and saw Sajina Bibi affix her thumb impression on the deed. DW-1 
and DW-2 also stated that the defendants took possession of the land 
after purchase and are still in possession. 
 
Upon a careful evaluation of the evidence, the learned trial court 
found that the plaintiffs failed to prove the alleged death of Sajina 
Bibi prior to execution of the deed. It further held that Exhibit–3 was a 
concocted and unproved document since its alleged author disowned 
it. The court observed that under Section 60 of the Registration Act, 

<<PAGE 4>>
4 
 
1908, a registered deed carries a statutory presumption of genuineness 
and due execution, which the plaintiffs utterly failed to rebut. It also 
found that the defendants’ possession corroborated the genuineness of 
the transaction and accordingly dismissed the suit on 27.10.2008. 
 
On appeal, the learned Joint District Judge, 3rd Court, Sylhet, in Title 
Appeal No. 144 of 2008, reversed the judgment by relying mainly on 
Exhibit–3 and concluded that Sajina Bibi had died before execution, 
rendering the deed void ab initio. It also appears that certain additional 
documents were taken into consideration without following the 
mandatory procedure of Order 41 Rule 27 CPC and without giving the 
defendants an opportunity to rebut or cross-examine. 
 
Being aggrieved, the defendants as petitioners moved this Court and 
obtained the present Rule. 
 
Mr. M. F. Ahmed, learned Advocate for the petitioners, submits that 
the appellate court’s judgment is a clear case of misreading of 
evidence and non-consideration of material facts. He submits that 
Exhibit–3 was rightly rejected by the trial court as its alleged author 
denied authorship, rendering it inadmissible under Section 67 of the 
Evidence Act, 1872. The appellate court, he contends, acted beyond 
jurisdiction in admitting additional documents without satisfying the 
preconditions of Order 41 Rule 27 CPC.  

<<PAGE 5>>
5 
 
He further submits that DW-1 and DW-2, including an attesting 
witness, proved the due execution and registration of the deed, and 
that under Section 60 of the Registration Act a registered deed enjoys 
a presumption of genuineness unless rebutted by cogent evidence, 
which was lacking here. He lastly submits that the suit, filed after 
eight years, is barred by limitation and not maintainable as a bare suit 
for cancellation under Section 39 of the Specific Relief Act, 1877 
without a prayer for declaration and possession. 
 
Per contra, Mr. Ali Ahsan Mullah, learned Advocate for the opposite 
parties, supports the appellate decree and submits that the appellate 
court rightly found that Sajina Bibi had died before 27.10.1992, and 
therefore the deed was void. He argues that the presumption under 
Section 60 of the Registration Act is rebuttable and that the appellate 
court exercised its power to admit additional evidence for doing 
complete justice. 
 
Upon hearing the parties and examining the records, this Court finds 
that revisional jurisdiction under Section 115 of the Code of Civil 
Procedure is limited to correcting jurisdictional errors and material 
irregularities resulting in miscarriage of justice. It does not permit 
substitution of factual findings unless those findings are perverse or 
based on no evidence. 

<<PAGE 6>>
6 
 
It is well-settled that a registered document carries a statutory 
presumption of genuineness under Section 60 of the Registration Act, 
1908. It was consistently held that unless the execution of a registered 
document is specifically disproved by credible evidence, courts must 
presume due execution and validity. The burden under Sections 101–
103 of the Evidence Act squarely lies upon the person who alleges 
forgery or posthumous execution.In the instant case, the plaintiffs 
utterly failed to discharge this burden.  
 
The so-called death certificate (Exhibit–3) was disowned by its 
alleged author, PW-3, and therefore carries no evidentiary value under 
Sections 67 and 68 of the Evidence Act. No other reliable evidence 
was produced to establish the date of death. In contrast, DW-1 and 
DW-2, including an attesting witness, testified clearly that Sajina Bibi 
was alive at the time of execution and voluntarily affixed her thumb 
impression on the deed. Their testimony, coupled with undisputed 
possession by the defendants, strongly supports the genuineness of the 
transaction. 
 
The appellate court, while reversing the well-reasoned judgment of 
the trial court, however, relied solely on an alleged death certificate 
purportedly issued by the Chairman, overlooking that the said 
document was neither exhibited nor marked as an exhibit, and the 

<<PAGE 7>>
7 
 
issuer Chairman was never examined. By disregarding the credible 
testimony of the defence witnesses and basing its conclusion on no 
legal evidence, the appellate court’s finding is manifestly perverse and 
unsustainable in law, committed a manifest error by relying on an 
unproved document and admitting additional evidence in violation of 
Order 41 Rule 27 CPC. It is now firmly settled that additional 
evidence at the appellate stage can be admitted only upon satisfaction 
of the conditions enumerated therein and after granting the opposite 
party an opportunity of rebuttal; failure to do so renders the judgment 
vitiated by material irregularity. 
 
The suit also suffers from the bar of limitation. The deed was 
executed in 1992, whereas the suit was filed in 2000. Under Article 91 
of the Limitation Act, 1908, a suit for cancellation of a document on 
the ground of fraud must be filed within three years from the date of 
discovery of the fraud. PW-1 admitted that the plaintiffs were aware 
of the deed much earlier and could not satisfactorily explain the long 
delay. Hence, the suit is hopelessly time-barred. 
 
Further, as the plaintiffs were admittedly out of possession, their bare 
suit for cancellation under Section 39 of the Specific Relief Act, 1877, 
without a prayer for declaration of title or recovery of possession, is 

<<PAGE 8>>
8 
 
not maintainable. Where the plaintiff is out of possession, a simple 
suit for cancellation cannot be entertained. 
 
It is a settled proposition that the sanctity of a registered deed cannot 
be lightly disturbed on mere suspicion or hearsay evidence. The courts 
must insist upon clear, cogent, and convincing proof of fraud or 
forgery. The appellate court’s findings being based on inadmissible 
and unproved evidence, its decree is clearly perverse and occasioned a 
failure of justice. 
 
On an overall assessment, this Court finds that the learned trial court 
made a lawful and well-reasoned appreciation of the evidence, 
whereas the appellate court exceeded its jurisdiction, misread the 
evidence, and relied upon an unproved document. Its judgment, 
therefore, cannot stand in the eye of law. 
Accordingly, the Rule is made absolute. 
The judgment and decree dated 25.09.2012 (decree signed on 
01.10.2012) passed by the learned Joint District Judge, 3rd Court, 
Sylhet in Title Appeal No. 144 of 2008 is hereby set aside. 
 
The judgment and decree dated 27.10.2008 (decree signed on 
03.11.2008) passed by the learned Senior Assistant Judge, Sadar, 

<<PAGE 9>>
9 
 
Sylhet, in Title Suit No. 166 of 2000 (renumbered as Title Suit No. 49 
of 2004) is restored. Consequently, the suit stands dismissed. 
The interim order of stay stands vacated.  
There shall be no order as to costs. 
Let a copy of this judgment be transmitted to the court below along 
with the Lower Court Records at once. 
 
(Justice Md. Toufiq Inam) 
 
 
Ashraf/ABO. 
","The PETITIONERS, contesting defendants, challenged an appellate decree that reversed a trial-court dismissal and cancelled a registered sale deed on the finding that the executant had died before execution. The plaintiff-OPPOSITE PARTIES relied on an alleged death certificate and claimed the deed was forged. The PETITIONERS relied on the presumption of genuineness attaching to registration, attesting-witness testimony, possession, the disowning of the certificate by its alleged author, limitation under Article 91 of the Limitation Act, 1908, and the maintainability of a bare cancellation suit under section 39 of the Specific Relief Act, 1877. They also objected to additional evidence admitted without complying with Order XLI rule 27 of the Code of Civil Procedure.",Evidence Act 1872,ALLOWED,"UNCERTAIN: the decision rests on several statutes, but the evidentiary burden is central. The court held that the OPPOSITE PARTIES failed to rebut the presumption of due execution of a registered deed and that the alleged death certificate had no evidentiary value because its author disowned it. The appellate court relied on unproved material and admitted additional evidence without the required procedure or rebuttal opportunity. The suit was also time-barred and, as a bare cancellation claim by parties out of possession, not maintainable. The trial court’s dismissal was therefore restored.",42f4ae5bda22301820e2612e100268c152f06c836fcf03ac3c2d0421251953f4
1550-1568,Civil Revision No. 3976 of 2024,,"
