1 
 
District-Thakurgaon. 
IN THE SUPREME COURT OF BANGLADESH 
HIGH COURT DIVISION 
(CIVIL REVISIONAL JURISDICTION) 
Present: 
Mr. Justice Md. Toufiq Inam 
Civil Revision No. 3976 of 2024. 
                     
Md. Roficul Islam @ Md. Rofujul Islam and others. 
                       ---- Pre-emptee-Appellant-Petitioners. 
                  -Versus- 
Md. Solayman Ali. 
             ---- Pre-emptor-Respondent-Opposite Party. 
Ms. Mahfuza Chowdhury, Advocate  
          ---- For the Pre-emptee-Appellant-Petitioners. 
Mr. Md. Jahangir Kamal, Advocate 
     ---- For the Pre-emptor-Respondent-Opposite Party. 
Heard On: 26.10.2025. 
                  And 
Judgment Delivered On: 28.10. 2025. 
 
 
 
  
Md. Toufiq Inam, J. 
This Rule, at the instance of the petitioners as pre-emptees, was issued 
calling upon the opposite parties to show cause as to why the 
impugned judgment and order dated 02.07.2024 passed by the learned 
Additional District Judge, Thakurgaon in Miscellaneous Appeal No. 
02 of 2019, affirming those dated 30.10.2008 passed by the learned 
Assistant Judge, Pirganj, Thakurgaon in Miscellaneous Case No. 46 of 
2008 allowing pre-emption, should not be set aside and/or such other 
or further order or orders passed as to this Court may seem fit and 
proper. 

<<PAGE 2>>
2 
 
The pre-emptees-petitioners’ case, in short, is that the land described 
in Schedule “Ka” of the plaint was originally recorded in the name of 
one MoulaBaks in S.A. Khatian Nos. 532 and 534. MoulaBaks died 
leaving behind his wife Gulzan, two sons- Habil and Ibrahimand two 
daughters- Samijan and Sabina. The S.A. records were accordingly 
prepared in their names after his death. 
 
The pre-emptor No. 1 is the son of Habil, while pre-emptor No. 2 is 
the daughter of Ibrahim. The pre-emptee No. 1 claims to have 
received by way of hiba-bil-ewaz 9.5 decimals from Plot No. 534 and 
10.25 decimals from Plot No. 532 from his mother Dahijan Bewa by 
registered deed No. 17882, and also purchased 32.5 decimals from 
Bhuban Ali and Saiyed Ali by registered deed No. 10426. 
 
The pre-emptors alleged that pre-emptee Nos. 5 and 6 secretly sold 86 
decimals of land out of the said plots to pre-emptees Nos. 1–4 through 
a registered sale deed No. 6715 dated 09.07.2008 without their 
knowledge, although the pre-emptors were co-sharers by inheritance 
in the holding. Upon learning of the sale and obtaining a certified 
copy of the deed on 20.08.2008, the pre-emptors filed the present pre-
emption case on 07.09.2008, depositing Tk. 1,89,506/- including 
consideration, compensation, and interest. 
 

<<PAGE 3>>
3 
 
The contesting pre-emptees-petitioners, on the other hand, denied the 
material averments and asserted that the suit was not maintainable, 
contending that the S.A. recorded owners were different, being 
Hafizuddin, Khamir Uddin, Mojiran, Amesha Khatun, and Sanjani 
Bibi under C.S. Khatians Nos. 54 and 443. They claimed title through 
various purchases and also alleged that pre-emptee No. 1 himself had 
sold land from the same holding earlier and, therefore, had no right to 
pre-empt. They further contended that the sale was made in 
consultation with the pre-emptors, who thus waived their right of pre-
emption under the principles of waiver, estoppel, and acquiescence. 
 
The learned trial court, upon careful evaluation of oral and 
documentary evidence, framed several issues for determination and 
recorded specific findings, inter alia, that: 
a) The pre-emptors are co-sharers by inheritance in the suit 
holding as descendants of MoulaBaks whose name appears in 
S.A. Khatian Nos. 532 and 534. 
b) The case was filed within limitation under Section 96(10) of the 
State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950. 
c) The case was not bad for defect of parties. 
d) The plea of waiver and estoppel was unsubstantiated by 
evidence. 

<<PAGE 4>>
4 
 
e) The pre-emptees failed to prove that they were co-sharers in the 
S.A. recorded holding, as the C.S. Khatians they relied upon 
had long been superseded by the S.A. record.Accordingly, the 
trial court allowed the pre-emption case in favour of the pre-
emptors. 
 
The learned appellate court, upon a re-appraisal of the entire record, 
concurred with the trial court’s findings and further held that the pre-
emptors, being co-sharers by inheritance, had an indefeasible statutory 
right of pre-emption under Section 96 of the SAT Act, 1950, and that 
the pre-emptees’ plea of co-sharership and waiver was devoid of 
merit. Consequently, the appeal was dismissed. 
 
Being aggrieved, the pre-emptees as petitioners moved this Court and 
obtained the present Rule, which is now taken up for disposal. 
 
Mrs. Mahfuza Chowdhury, learned Advocate appearing for Mr. AZM 
Fariduzzaman Farhad, learned Advocatesubmits on behalf of the pre-
emptees-petitioners that both courts below misread the evidence and 
failed to consider that the pre-emptors had full knowledge of the sale 
and even participated in its negotiation, thereby forfeiting their right 
to pre-emption. She further contends that the pre-emptee No. 1, being 
heir of Ismail Hossain who purchased land from the same holding, is a 

<<PAGE 5>>
5 
 
co-sharer by inheritance and, therefore, the pre-emption claim is 
legally untenable. 
 
Per contra, Mr. Md. Jahangir Kamal, learned Advocate for the pre-
emptors-opposite parties, supports the concurrent findings and 
submits that both courts below correctly found the pre-emptors to be 
co-sharers by inheritance in the S.A. holding and that the sale was 
made to strangers without serving notice under Section 89 of the SAT 
Act. He contends that the plea of waiver was rightly rejected as a mere 
afterthought unsupported by any credible or independent evidence. 
 
This Court has meticulously examined the revisional application, the 
impugned judgments, and the evidence on record. 
 
It is well settled that concurrent findings of fact based on proper 
appraisal of evidence should not be disturbed in revision unless shown 
to be perverse, based on no evidence, or resulting in a miscarriage of 
justice. In the present case, both the courts below have concurrently 
and correctly held that the pre-emptors are co-sharers by inheritance 
as descendants of Moula Baks, whose name appears in the S.A. 
record. The pre-emptees’ attempt to trace co-sharership from the 
obsolete C.S. record is misconceived in law, as the S.A. record 
supersedes the earlier entries and governs the present holding. 

<<PAGE 6>>
6 
 
The plea of waiver or negotiation raised by the pre-emptees is wholly 
unsubstantiated. No witness or documentary proof was produced to 
show any active participation by the pre-emptors in the sale 
transaction. The mere assertion of knowledge or alleged discussion 
does not amount to waiver of a statutory right. As held in Fazaruddin 
vs. Maijuddin and others, 44 DLR (AD) 62, the right of pre-emption 
conferred upon a co-sharer by inheritance is a statutory right, which 
cannot be defeated by mere oral assertions of acquiescence or 
informal consent unless supported by convincing and unequivocal 
evidence. 
 
Regarding limitation, the record shows that the pre-emption case was 
filed within two months of the date of knowledge and well within 
three years of registration of the impugned deed, satisfying the dual 
requirements of Section 96(10) of the SAT Act, 1950. 
 
A co-sharer by inheritance in an S.A. recorded holding retains a 
statutory right of pre-emption under Section 96 of the SAT Act, 1950, 
which can only be defeated by clear and convincing proof of waiver 
or participation in the sale transaction. Mere knowledge or alleged 
negotiation, without corroborating evidence, does not constitute 
waiver. Concurrent findings based on proper appreciation of S.A. 

<<PAGE 7>>
7 
 
records and evidence are not open to interference in revision under 
Section 115 CPC. 
 
Thus, the findings of both courts below are founded upon correct 
appreciation of the evidence and the governing statutory principles. 
The pre-emptees-petitioners have failed to demonstrate any error of 
law, jurisdictional defect, or misreading/non-reading of evidence that 
would justify interference under Section 115 of the Code of Civil 
Procedure. 
Accordingly, this Court finds no substance in the Rule. 
In the result, the Rule is discharged. 
The judgment and order dated 02.07.2024 passed by the learned 
Additional District Judge, Thakurgaon in Miscellaneous Appeal No. 
02 of 2019, affirming those dated 30.10.2008 passed by the learned 
Assistant Judge, Pirganj, Thakurgaon in Miscellaneous Case No. 46 of 
2008, are hereby affirmed. 
There shall be no order as to costs. 
Let the lower court records be sent down at once. 
 
(Justice Md. Toufiq Inam)  
 
 
Ashraf/ABO. 
","The PETITIONERS, pre-emptees, challenged concurrent orders permitting pre-emption of a registered sale of agricultural land. They argued that the pre-emptor-OPPOSITE PARTY had knowledge of and participated in the sale, thereby waiving the right of pre-emption, and that one PETITIONER was himself a co-sharer by inheritance. The pre-emptor-OPPOSITE PARTY relied on co-sharership by inheritance in the S.A. recorded holding, absence of notice under section 89 of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, and limitation under section 96(10). The revision concerns whether the concurrent findings on co-sharership, waiver and limitation disclose any error warranting interference, and whether the statutory right of pre-emption was properly enforced.",Other: State Acquisition and Tenancy Act 1950,REFUSED,"The court held that the pre-emptor-OPPOSITE PARTY was a co-sharer by inheritance in the S.A. recorded holding, which superseded the obsolete C.S. entries relied on by the PETITIONERS. The OPPOSITE PARTY’S statutory right of pre-emption under section 96 could be defeated only by clear and convincing proof of waiver or participation, and no such evidence was produced. The case satisfied the dual limitation requirements of section 96(10). The concurrent findings were based on proper appreciation of evidence and disclosed no perversity, jurisdictional defect or miscarriage of justice justifying revisional interference.",6f26e577c148207b3f5169afbad3e0e2fabc8c1f7c792c87c6299a0a74330e2e
1550-1569,Civil Revision No. 6836 of 2023,2025-10-27,"
