","Two APPELLANTS, a project director and a local agent of a foreign supplier, challenged convictions under sections 409 and 109 of the Penal Code, 1860 arising from procurement of film-development equipment. The prosecution alleged that Chinese-origin equipment was supplied instead of USA or Japanese equipment and that public funds were misappropriated. The APPELLANTS argued that the tender contained no “made in” condition, that the manufacturer had relocated production, that the managing director approved acceptance, and that the equipment was used without complaint. The STATE and anti-corruption RESPONDENT relied on an enquiry report, alleged model and origin variations and the receiving committee’s objections. The appeals concern whether criminal breach of trust and abetment were proved.",Penal Code 1860,ALLOWED,"The court found that the tender did not require equipment “made in” the USA or Japan, and the supplier’s offer referred to origin rather than a manufacturing-location condition. The manufacturer remained American or Japanese, and the supplier’s explanation about relocation had been accepted by the managing director before payment and acceptance. The equipment was installed and used without complaint. No expert or mother-company report verified the alleged mismatch, and the investigating officer had excluded the approving managing director. The trial court also ignored the defence statement under section 342. The prosecution therefore failed to prove the charges beyond reasonable doubt.",10423c8055028079360c15fef82c043665621a31db6688707acf98aa6ca37d0c
