IN THE SUPREME COURT OF BANGLADESH 
HIGH COURT DIVISION 
(CIVIL REVISIONAL JURISDICTION) 
                  Present: 
Mr. Justice Md. Toufiq Inam 
Civil Revision No. 1116 of 2025. 
Md. Delowar Hossain alias Saijul Islam and others.   
                                          ---- Plaintiffs-Petitioners. 
   
 
 
  -Versus- 
Md. Kalam Mollah and others.  
                              ---- Defendant-Opposite Parties. 
  
Mr. Md. Azim Uddin, Advocate.   
        ---- For the Plaintiffs-Petitioners.  
Mr. Md. Shahidul Islam, Senior Advocate with  
Mr. S.M. Zakir Hossain, Advocate.  
                               ---- For the Plaintiffs-Opposite Parties. 
 
 
 Heard and Judgment Delivered On: 26.10.2025. 
 
 
Md. Toufiq Inam, J. 
Upon granting leave, this Rule was issued calling upon the opposite 
party Nos. 1–3, 11, 19, 32 and 33 to show cause as to why the order 
dated 18.11.2024 passed by the learned District Judge, Barishal in 
Civil Revision No. 95 of 2024, summarily dismissing the application 
and thereby affirming the order dated 08.09.2024 passed by the 
learned Assistant Judge, Muladi, Barishal in Title Suit No. 96 of 2024 
rejecting the plaintiffs’ application for stay, should not be set aside 
and/or such other or further order or orders passed as to this Court 
may seem fit and proper. 
 

<<PAGE 2>>
 
Page # 2
The petitioners, as plaintiffs, instituted Title Suit No. 96 of 2024 
seeking (i) declaration of their title over the land described in the 
schedule “Ka”, and (ii) a further declaration that the judgment and 
decree dated 27.03.1967 (decree drawn on 09.04.1967) passed by the 
learned Sub-Judge, Barishal in Title Suit No. 14 of 1967 were obtained 
by fraud and are not binding upon them. 
 
Their case is that the defendants’ predecessors procured the 1967 
decree by practicing fraud upon the court. The present plaintiffs Nos. 
1 and 2, namely Md. Delwar Hossain alias Saijul Islam and Md. 
Anowar Hossain, were minors at the relevant time and no guardian ad 
litem had been appointed to represent them. It is alleged that the so-
called compromise petition was forged and fabricated, and therefore, 
the resultant decree is void ab initio. 
 
During pendency of the present suit, the plaintiffs sought an order 
staying operation of the said 1967 decree until disposal of the suit. 
The trial court rejected the prayer, and the revisional court, concurring 
with that view, dismissed Civil Revision No. 95 of 2024. Hence, the 
present Rule. 
 
Mr. Md. Azim Uddin, learned Advocate for the petitioners, submits 
that fraud vitiates all judicial acts. Since the 1967 decree was founded 
upon a fraudulent compromise to which the minor plaintiffs were not 
parties, it is a nullity in law and non est in the eye of the court. He 

<<PAGE 3>>
 
Page # 3
argues that unless operation of the decree is stayed, the plaintiffs will 
suffer irreparable loss and the very purpose of the pending suit will be 
frustrated. He contends that fraud unravels everything and no person 
can be allowed to benefit from his own wrong. 
 
Per contra, Mr. Md. Shahidul Islam, learned Senior Advocate for the 
opposite parties, on the other hand, submits that the plaintiffs have 
approached the court after an inordinate lapse of more than fifty-seven 
years. A decree passed by a competent court remains valid until it is 
duly set aside, and mere allegations of fraud cannot justify suspension 
of its operation. He further submits that the plaintiffs’ plea of minority 
and absence of a guardian ad litem are disputed questions of fact to be 
established by evidence at trial. Both the courts below, he argues, 
exercised judicial discretion properly, and their concurrent findings do 
not warrant interference under Section 115(4) of the Code of Civil 
Procedure 
 
This court has considered the rival submissions and examined the 
materials on record. The decree in Title Suit No. 14 of 1967 was 
passed more than five decades ago, and its validity is now being 
challenged collaterally in Title Suit No. 96 of 2024. 
 

<<PAGE 4>>
 
Page # 4
The central question is whether a civil court can stay operation of an 
old decree merely upon allegations of fraud pending adjudication of a 
separate declaratory suit. 
 
It is a settled proposition that a decree of a competent court is 
presumed valid unless and until set aside in due course of law. The 
principle of finality of litigation requires that judgments, once 
rendered, should not be lightly disturbed. Fraud must be pleaded with 
particularity and proved by clear and cogent evidence, not by general 
allegations. 
 
The plaintiffs’ claim that they were minors at the relevant time and 
unrepresented by any guardian ad litem raises a mixed question of 
fact and law which cannot be decided without evidence. The 
appointment (or absence) of a guardian ad litem must be ascertained 
from the record of the earlier suit, and this can only be examined at 
the stage of full trial, not on an interlocutory application. Moreover, 
the maxim fraus omnia corrumpit (fraud unravels everything), does 
not mean that a mere allegation of fraud automatically invalidates a 
decree. It must first be established through a proper proceeding.  
 
Hence, until the plaintiffs succeed in proving their allegations of fraud 
in the pending suit, the decree of 1967 continues to have binding 
force. The courts below were therefore justified in refusing to stay its 
operation. This Court finds no illegality, irregularity, or jurisdictional 

<<PAGE 5>>
 
Page # 5
error in the orders impugned. Both courts applied correct principles of 
law in exercising their discretion. 
 
A decree of a competent civil court, even if alleged to have been 
obtained by fraud, retains its binding force and legal effect until it is 
set aside through proper judicial proceedings. Mere allegations of 
fraud, without adjudication, cannot suspend or nullify the operation of 
such a decree. Relief by way of stay can be granted only upon prima 
facie proof of fraud striking at the root of jurisdiction; otherwise, the 
doctrine of finality of judgment must prevail. 
 
In view of the foregoing discussion and settled legal principles, this 
Court finds no reason to interfere with the concurrent findings of the 
courts below. 
 
Accordingly, the Rule stands discharged. 
There shall be no order as to costs. 
 
The order of status quo, stands vacated. 
 
Let a copy of this judgment be transmitted to the courts below. 
 
(Justice Md. Toufiq Inam) 
 
Sayed.B.O 
","The PETITIONERS, plaintiffs in a declaratory suit, challenged concurrent orders declining to stay the operation of a 1967 compromise decree while their suit alleging fraud was pending. They claimed that two PETITIONERS were minors unrepresented by a guardian ad litem and that the compromise petition was forged, making the decree a nullity. They argued that fraud vitiates judicial acts and that irreparable loss would follow without a stay. The OPPOSITE PARTIES relied on the decree’s long-standing validity, the delay of more than fifty-seven years, and the need to prove minority and absence of representation through evidence. The revision concerns interlocutory suspension of an old decree and the scope of revisional interference.",Not stated,REFUSED,"The court held that a decree of a competent court is presumed valid and retains binding force until set aside through proper proceedings. Allegations of fraud must be pleaded with particularity and proved by clear evidence; they do not automatically suspend a decree. The PETITIONERS’ minority and lack of a guardian ad litem were mixed questions of fact and law requiring trial, not interlocutory determination. Because no prima facie fraud striking at jurisdiction was established, the doctrine of finality prevailed. The concurrent discretionary orders disclosed no illegality, irregularity or jurisdictional error warranting interference.",b3aade1934da5999da7349657c8f9e5d000ee9c1fda760450e7675688ead47c0
