Sharmeen Sultana. By those deeds, they transferred a total of 
28.40 decimals of land at the rate of Tk. 5,50,000.00/-. But the 
defendants received an excess amount of Tk. 80,18,000/- in 
total. Meanwhile, the defendant executed a power of attorney 
dated 25.07.2019 in favour of the plaintiff. So, the defendants 
agreed to execute another deed of sale for 14.72 decimals of 
land in favour of the plaintiff in exchange for the amount of Tk. 
80,18,000/-, which they have already received. Accordingly, the 
plaintiff demanded a Kabala from the defendant on 01.10.2021. 
Rather, the defendants refused to execute the kabala deed as 
such, the instant suit. 
The petitioners herein, as defendants, contested the suit 
by filing a joint written statement, denying all material 
allegations against them. They contended, inter alia, that the 
agreement between the parties had expired and the suit was 

<<PAGE 4>>
 
4 
time-barred, and that the plaintiff had not deposited the 
remaining consideration money as stated in the plaint. So, the 
plaint is liable to be rejected. 
During the pendency of the suit, the defendants filed an 
application under Order VII Rule 11 of the Code of Civil 
Procedure for rejecting the plaint. The plaintiff contested the 
same by filing a written objection and also filed an application 
under Order VII Rule 17 of the Code of Civil Procedure to amend 
the plaint. 
Subsequently, the learned Joint District Judge, 1st Court, 
Noakhali, by the Judgment and decree dated 19.10.2022, 
rejected the plaint upon allowing the application under Order 
VII Rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure, as well as rejected 
the amendment application.  
Being aggrieved by and dissatisfied with the above 
Judgment and decree dated 19.10.2022, the plaintiff, as 
appellant, preferred Title Appeal No. 195 of 2022 before the 
learned District Judge, Noakhali. Eventually, the learned 
Special Judge, Noakhali, by the Judgment and decree dated 
15.10.2023, allowed the appeal, setting aside the Judgment and 
decree of the trial Court, and restored the suit. 

<<PAGE 5>>
 
5 
 
Being aggrieved by and dissatisfied with the above 
Judgment and decree dated 15.10.2023, the defendants, as 
petitioners, preferred this Civil Revision under Section 115 (1) of 
the Code of Civil Procedure before this court and obtained the 
instant Rule with an order of stay extended from time to time.  
 
Mr. Sherder Abul Hossain, the learned Counsel appearing 
on behalf of the petitioners, submits that as per the plaint, the 
consideration value of the land has been fixed at Tk. 
5,22,50,000/-, and the plaintiff paid Tk. 2,14,58,500/-, leaving 
a remaining balance consideration amount of Tk. 3,07,91,500/-
. The plaintiff did not deposit the balance consideration money 
at the time of filing the suit, which is a condition precedent to 
filing a suit for specific performance of a contract;  that the 
contract for sale was registered on 02.10.2018, stipulating the 
period of performance within 1(one) year, i.e., on or before 
01.10.2020, as per provision of Section 54A of the Transfer of 
Property Act read with Article 113 of the Limitation Act but the 
present suit was filed on 21.10.2020 which is contrary to the 
aforesaid provision of law thus the appellate court below 
allowed the appeal, therefore,  committed an error of law 
resulted error in the decision occasioning failure of justice. In 

<<PAGE 6>>
 
6 
his contention, he referred to the case of Abul Kalam (Md) Vs. 
Md. Mohiuddin and others reported in 69 DLR (AD) 239.  
Mr. Md. Omar Farouq, the learned Senior Advocate 
appearing on behalf of the plaintiff-opposite party, submits that 
the general principle of deposit of consideration money in full at 
the time of filing suit for specific performance of a contract is 
not applicable in the present suit because the plaintiff paid full 
of amount of TK. 5, 87,78, 000/-to the defendant against the 
total land price and extra amount of TK. 20,00,000/- has been 
paid to the defendant through the banking channel in 
continuation of the agreement moreover, the defendants 
admitted some transactions as per terms and condition of the 
agreement even after expiry of the period of agreement as 
stipulated in its which clearly saves the suit from being barred 
by limitation; that the plaintiff has brought the suit for part of 
the property schedule in the agreement because the rest of the 
land are not in a position to be registered due to record of that 
part is not up-to-date and some civil litigation are pending for 
that part of the land moreover, as per terms and condition of 
the agreement transfer deed may be executed for part of the 
schedule property. 

<<PAGE 7>>
 
7 
I have anxiously considered the submissions advanced by 
both parties, perused the Judgment of the courts below, and 
oral and documentary evidence on the records.  
It manifests from the record that the plaintiff instituted 
the instant suit for specific performance of the contract. The 
plaintiff assailed in the plaint is that the defendant entered into 
a registered sale agreement on 08.04.2014 to sell 102 decimals 
of land at TK. 5,50,000.00 per decimal, the plaintiff has already 
paid TK. 5,61,00,000.00 as an advance of sale proceeds through 
the banking channel. It is also claimed that the plaintiff 
expended TK. 1,04,50,000.00 for developing the land and 
securing possession by erecting a boundary wall in furtherance 
of converting the same into a modern housing project. The 
defendant executed several deeds in favour of various persons 
at the plaintiff's instance, transferred land, and, finally, on 
12.08.2021, the defendant transferred the property according to 
the sale agreement. The defendant received TK. 80,18,500.00/- 
from the plaintiff, and an agreement was made to execute a 
deed for the sale of 14.72 decimals. However, on 01.10.2021, 
the defendant refused to comply with the agreement. The 
learned Joint District Judge, 1st Court Noakhali, rejected the 
plaint, by the Judgment and decree dated 19.10.2022, 

<<PAGE 8>>
 
8 
considering the application filed by the defendant under Order 
7, Rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure. 
It is the settled provision of law that in deciding an 
application under Order VII Rule 11 of the Code of Civil 
Procedure, the court is not permitted to travel beyond the 
averment made in the plaint. In the instant case it appears from 
the sale agreement that there are some vital proviso were 
inserted in the agreement that- (i) the agreement is executed 
apparently for one year; (ii) that the executants i.e. defendants 
were under obligation of supplying necessary papers and 
documents; (iii) that the defendants undertook to execute sale 
deed in favour of the plaintiff or any other person as the 
instance of the plaintiff; (iv) that consideration money might be 
paid in part or full and accordingly the sale deed might also be 
for part or full of the schedule property as per ratio of the 
payment of consideration money.  
It transpired from the record that the suit was filed on 
21.102021, although the suit was required to be filed on or 
before 02.10.2020 as per the proviso so enumerated in Article 
113 of the 1st Schedule of the Limitation Act. However, upon 
compassionate consideration of the plaint, it appears that the 
defendant executed several deeds in favour of various persons 

<<PAGE 9>>
 
9 
at the plaintiff's instance, transferred a portion of the suit land, 
and, lastly, transferred a portion of the suit land by deed dated 
12.08.2021, as per the sale agreement and at the instance of 
the plaintiff. Therefore, it transpired that the transaction was 
done after expiry of the sale agreement, as the sale agreement 
has a condition that the defendant undertook to execute the 
sale deed in favor of the plaintiff or third persons at the instance 
of the plaintiff. In this regard, the learned Judge of the appellate 
court below says that:- 
""On perusal of the documents filed on behalf of the 
plaintiff, it is apparently found that some deeds of 
sale were executed by the defendants for some 
portions of the land of the schedule of the agreement 
in favour of some third parties, in which the plaintiff 
is found to be a witness. These deeds, accompanied 
by the banking transactions between the parties as 
asserted in the plaint are good circumstantial 
evidence of proof that the plaintiff was activ player in 
those sales and transactions which also gives rise of 
a reasonable presumption that plaintiff arranged 
those sales and monetary transaction as per the 
condition of the agreement that the defendant 

<<PAGE 10>>
 
10
 
undertook to execute sale deed in favour of the 
plaintiff or any other person at the instance of the 
plaintiff. If the plaintiff may prove these facts as per 
his assertion in the plaint, that may give rise of 
another presumption that the parties of the 
agreement 
are acting 
in 
continuation 
of 
the 
agreement even after expiry of the period of the 
agreement. The plaintiff clearly claims in the plaintiff 
that he had the last transaction before filing of the 
suit on 12.08.2021. But the court below has come to 
a conclusion that those transactions were made with 
third party beyond the agreement. We should keep 
in mind that since the agreement has a condition 
that the defendant undertook to execute sale deed in 
favour of the plaintiff or any other third person at 
the instance of the plaintiff the plaintiff should be 
given chance to prove that the transation after expiry 
of agreement was done in pursuance of the 
agreement and that was reasonably suficient to give 
rise in the mind of the plaintiff that the agreement 
remains 
in 
continuation. 
Actually, 
all 
these 
assertions of the plaintiff-appellant are matter of fact 

<<PAGE 11>>
 
11
 
which can never be determined without adducing 
and producing evidence at the time of trial of the 
suit.""  
We have already noticed that the defendant executed a 
sale agreement with the plaintiff to sell 102 decimals of land 
and fix the price of each decimal for Tk.5,50,000.00/-, so the 
total cost of the land is Tk.5,61,00,000.00/-, out of which an 
amount of Tk.5,50,00,000.00 was paid for several deeds in 
favour of several persons for several portions of land at the 
instance of the plaintiff. It also manifests that the defendant 
filed a case in the Land Survey Tribunal for correction of the 
record for 14.53 decimals of the suit land, out of the total suit 
land; a good portion of the suit land under the agreement were 
not recorded in the name of the vendors in full. A portion of the 
property has not yet been mutated in the names of the 
defendants. Some portions are subject matter of the Land 
Survey Tribunal for the correction of the record of right. 
Further, it appears from the terms and conditions of the sale 
agreement that the consideration money may be paid in part or 
in full, and accordingly, the sale deed might also be for part or 
all of the scheduled property as per the ratio of the payment of 
consideration money. Therefore, the defendant is found liable 

<<PAGE 12>>
 
12
 
under the agreement, which stipulates that he should supply 
up-to-date documents to the plaintiff for all properties within 
the agreed-upon timeframe. Consequently, the plaintiff is 
seeking relief for part of the land and has filed the suit after the 
expiry of the time stipulated in the sale agreement. Therefore, 
the principle of depositing the entire consideration money at the 
time of filing the suit for a specific performance of the contract 
is not applicable in the present suit, on the contrary, the instant 
suit is a suitable case to follow the principle laid down in the 
proviso so enumerated in Illustration (a) of section 15 of the 
Specific Relief Act, 1987.  
It is the settled proposition of law that a specific time is 
fixed for payment or for convenance, and it does not usually 
make the essence. This view gets support from the case of Amir 
Hossain Sowdagar vs. Md. Harunur Rashid and others reported 
in 65 DLR (AD)  130, wherein their Lordship of the Appellate 
Division observed that:- 
“On a plain reading of this deed, there is no gain 
saying that there is obligation on the part of the 
defendant to comply with the above conditions other 
than the last one, which shall be complied by the 
plaintiff. Though there is stipulation for completion 

<<PAGE 13>>
 
13
 
of the transaction within a period of 6(six) months, 
this time limit for payment was circumscribed by the 
fulfillment of the obligations by the defendant, who 
was not given unconditional power to rescind the 
agreement 
for 
non-payment 
of 
the 
balance 
consideration within six months. The defendant is 
under obligation to handover the vacant possession 
of the house except the shop and to deliver collateral 
documents of title and then to arrange for the 
execution of the tripartite deed with HBFC and the 
plaintiff. After fulfillment of these conditions, the 
question of by the payment of the balance 
consideration after adjustment of HBFC loan and 
advance would arise at the time of execution of the 
sale deed. He is also required to resolve the dispute 
regarding the shop, which appears from the recitals 
of the agreement that it is in the possession of the 
tenant, even though there is no such positive 
statement, other-wise the parties would not have 
used the expression 'settlement' of the same. 
Assuming that the plaintiff is required to pay the 
HBFC's outstanding loan but how he would pay 

<<PAGE 14>>
 
14
 
unless the defendant arranged with HBFC that the 
plaintiff would adjust the outstanding amount of 
loan on his behalf, and this could only be done if the 
tripartite deed was executed as per agreement, 
otherwise there was no need from providing such 
clause. It would be necessary for the purpose of 
adjustment of the outstanding loan to negotiate first 
with HBFC for the execution of tripartite deed so 
that the plaintiff would be able to adjust the balance 
amount of loan.” 
In the case reported in 65 DLR (AD) 130, their Lordship 
further held that:- 
“Section 55 of the Contract Act lays down the effect of 
failure to perform at fixed time in contracts in which time 
(a) is essential, or (b) is not essential, acceptance or 
performance at time other than that agreed upon. In 
Jamshed Khodaram Irani vs. Burjoriji Dhunjibhai, AIR 
1915 PC 83, it was provided in the agreement that the 
deed was to be prepared and received within two months 
from the date of execution; the balance consideration 
should be paid on the signing of the deed and, in default 
of payment within the stipulated time, the earnest money 

<<PAGE 15>>
 
15
 
would be forfeited and the vendor would have the right to 
sell the land. In the facts of the given case, their Lordship 
were of the opinion that section 55 of the Contract Act 
does not lay down any principle, as regards contracts to 
sell land, different from those which obtain under the 
common law of England. Under that law, equity, which 
governs the rights of the parties in cases of specific 
performance of contracts to sell land, looks not at the 
letter but at the substance of the contract in order to 
ascertain whether the parties, notwithstanding that they 
named a specific time within which completion was to 
take place, really and in substance intended more than 
that it should take place within a reasonable time. Specific 
performance of contract of that nature will be granted, 
although there has been a failure to keep the dates 
assigned by it, if justice can be cone between the parties, 
and if nothing in (a) the express stipulations of the parties, 
(b) the nature of the property, or (c) the surrounding 
circumstances make it inequitable to grant the relief. It 
was observed ""But to have this effect the language of the 
stipulation must show that the intention was to make the 
rights of the parties depend on the observance of the time 

<<PAGE 16>>
 
16
 
limits 
prescribed 
in 
a 
fashion 
which 
is 
unmistakable...............But equity will; not assist where 
there has been undue delay on the part of one party to the 
contract, and the other has given him reasonable nothing 
that he must complete within a definite time. Nor will it 
exercise its jurisdiction when the character of the property 
or other circumstances would render such exercise likely 
to result in injustice. In such cases, the circumstances 
themselves, apart from any question of expressed 
intention, exclude the jurisdiction."" 
In the case reported in 65 DLR (AD) 130, their Lordship 
also held that:- 
""In contracts for the sale of immovable property, the 
fact that a specific time is fixed for payment or for 
conveyance does not normally make time of the 
essence. Failure to pay at a fixed time is not, per se, 
sufficient to terminate the seller's duty to convey; 
and failure to convey on the exact date does not per 
se discharge the buyer. In such agreements, usually 
time is not the essence of the contract, the reason 
being that the law of equity, which governs rights of 
the parties in cases of specific performance of 

<<PAGE 17>>
 
17
 
contracts of sale of immovable property, looks not at 
the letter but at the substance of the agreement. The 
intention to treat time as the essence of the contract 
may be evidenced by circumstances which are 
sufficiently 
strong 
to 
displace 
the 
normal 
presumption that in a contract for sale of real estate, 
stipulation as to time is not the essence of the 
contract.""  
In view of the above, it is clear that, in determining the 
parties intention the court is required to consider the agreement 
at or before the date of execution, not afterwards. If the time is 
not made originally of the essence of the contract, one of the 
parties is not entitled afterwards to make it of the essence. 
Therefore, these subsequent fixations of time by one party could 
not be considered as the basis to infer the time of essence of the 
contract. In this regard, in the case of Green vs. Sevin (1879), 
the House of Lords held that:- 
“When time is not originally made of the essence of a 
contract for sale of land, one of the parties is not entitled 
afterwards by notice to make it of the essence, unless 
there has been some default or unreasonable delay by the 
other party.” 

<<PAGE 18>>
 
18
 
Notably, the instant case is founded purely on a finding of 
the mixed question of facts and law based on the correct 
evaluation of the facts and law therefore, in deciding the 
application for the rejection of the plaint, the court is not 
permitted to travel beyond the averments made in the plaint, 
and to determine the truth of the mixed question of law and 
fact, evidence is necessary, and this can be available only in the 
course of the trial of the suit.  
We have anxiously considered the case of Abul Kalam (Md) 
{Supra} wherein their Lordships of the Appellate Division  held 
that:- 
“We have considered the provision of section 21 A(b) 
of the Act. The language of the section is so 
unambiguous 
that 
it 
does 
not 
require 
any 
interpretation to come to conclusion that in case of 
failure of depositing the balance amount at the time 
of filing the suit for specific performance of the 
contract, the suit cannot be maintained. Even then, 
from the impugned Judgment and order, it appears 
that the High Court Division considered various 
decisions of this court and of the Indian jurisdiction 
and came to the finding that the deposit of the 

<<PAGE 19>>
 
19
 
balance consideration of the contract before filing a 
suit for specific performance of the contract is a 
condition precedent and that having not been done 
in the instant case, that suit was barred under the 
provision of section 21A(b) of the Act. Therefore, the 
plaint was liable to be rejected under Order VII, Rule 
11 of the Code of Civil Procedure. We find no error 
with the view taken by the High Court Division in 
view of the language used in section 21A(b) of the 
Act."" 
We are in full agreement with the decision of the above-
cited case. However, its case depends upon its own facts and 
circumstances. In the instant case, we have already noticed that 
the defendant executed a sale agreement with the plaintiff to 
sell 102 decimals of land and fix the price of each decimal for 
Tk.5,50,000.00/-, 
so 
the 
total 
cost 
of 
the 
land 
is 
Tk.5,61,00,000.00/-, 
out 
of 
which 
an 
amount 
of 
Tk.5,50,00,000.00/- was paid. Moreover, the defendant filed a 
case in the Land Survey Tribunal for correction of the record for 
14.53 decimals of land, out of the total land; a good portion of 
the suit lands under the agreement were not recorded in the 
name of the vendors in full. A portion of the property has not 

<<PAGE 20>>
 
20
 
yet been mutated in the names of the defendants. Some 
portions are subject matter of the Land Survey Tribunal for the 
correction of the record of right. Further, the terms and 
conditions of the sale agreement stipulate that the consideration 
money may be paid in part or in whole. Therefore, the principle 
of depositing the entire consideration money at the time of filing 
the suit for a specific performance of the contract is not 
applicable in the present suit.  
We have also perused the application filed by the plaintiff 
under Order VI Rule 17 of the Code of Civil Procedure. It is a 
well-established principle that the object of courts is to decide 
the rights of the parties and not to punish them for mistakes 
they make in the conduct of their cases by deciding otherwise 
than in accordance with their rights. If the error or mistake is 
not fraudulent or is not intended to overreach, the court ought 
to correct it if it cannot do injustice to the other side. The court 
does not exist for the sake of discipline but to decide matters in 
controversy. We do not regard such an amendment as a matter 
of favor or grace. It seems that the amendment will help 
determine the fundamental controversy. It is as much a matter 
of right for the plaintiffs to have the plaint corrected by this 
amendment. 

<<PAGE 21>>
 
21
 
Considering the above facts and circumstances, it appears 
that the learned Judge of the appellate court below rightly says 
that the trial court is found confined in the literal averment of 
the sale agreement regarding its period, which affects the order 
to be misconceived in selecting the decision of the higher court. 
However, the specific performance of the contract is an 
equitable relief. Many alternative remedies may be awarded in a 
suit for specific performance of the contract. Since the last 
transaction between the parties before the suit was filed is 
claimed to have occurred on 12.08.2021, that should be proved 
in the trial, as it is a matter of fact. So, under the facts and 
circumstances explained in the plaint as well as in the 
application for amendment of the plaint at this stage, the suit 
cannot be said to be barred by any law. It should be left for 
trial. Therefore, the relief sought by way of amendment of the 
plaint and the prayer to have permission to deposit the 
remaining consideration money should be allowed for the ends 
of justice under the law and the principle of equity. On the 
contrary, the trial court below misdirected and misconstrued 
the facts and circumstances of the case, and rejected the plaint; 
as such, it committed an error of law, resulting in an error in 
the decision and occasioning a failure of justice. 

<<PAGE 22>>
 
22
 
Considering the above facts, circumstances of the case, 
and discussions made herein above, we are of the firm view that 
the court of appeal below, considering all the material aspects of 
the matter, significantly and judiciously restored the suit in 
allowing the appeal and reversing the Judgment and decree of 
the trial court below. Consequently, it appears to me that the 
impugned Judgment and decree do not suffer from any legal 
infirmity, so the impugned Judgment is well-founded in 
accordance with law and based on the materials on record, 
which cannot be interfered with by this court exercising 
revisional power under Section 115 (1) of the Code of Civil 
Procedure. 
Resultantly, the Rule is discharged.  
The impugned Judgment and decree dated 15.10.2023, 
passed by the learned Special Judge, Noakhali, in Title Appeal 
No. 195 of 2022, restored the suit in allowing the appeal and 
reversing the Judgment and decree dated 19.10.2022 passed by 
the learned Joint District Judge, 1st Court, Noakhali. 
The order of status quo passed at the time of issuance of 
the Rule stands vacated. 
 
Communicate the Judgment and send down the Lower 
Court Records at once.  
 
