Kabir/BO 
","The PETITIONERS, vendors under a registered agreement for sale of land, challenged an appellate decree restoring a specific-performance suit after the trial court had ordered rejection of the plaint under Order VII rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure. The plaintiff-OPPOSITE PARTY claimed part performance, substantial payments, conveyances to third parties at his instance, and a balance entitlement to a smaller portion. The PETITIONERS argued that the suit was time-barred under Article 113 of the Limitation Act and that deposit of the balance consideration was a condition precedent under the Specific Relief Act. The OPPOSITE PARTY relied on continuing transactions, the PETITIONERS’ document obligations and the agreement’s part-payment and part-conveyance clauses. The revision concerns whether mixed questions of limitation, deposit and amendment should be decided at trial.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court held that an Order VII rule 11 application must be decided on the plaint averments alone and cannot resolve mixed questions of fact and law. The agreement permitted part payment and part conveyance, imposed document-supply obligations on the PETITIONERS, and was followed by transactions after the stated period, so time was not necessarily of the essence and the balance-deposit principle was inapplicable at this stage. The amendment sought would clarify the real controversy. The appellate court therefore correctly restored the suit for trial, and no revisional interference was warranted.",91817706da90b46e10d9298a2d402884ed8564f06880665daee2cd62e0a6c33b
